1
00:00:58,023 --> 00:01:02,619
[man] "In order to affect a timely halt
to deteriorating conditions

2
00:01:02,694 --> 00:01:05,288
and to ensure the common good,

3
00:01:05,364 --> 00:01:08,731
a state of emergency is declared
for these territories

4
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:11,428
by decree of Lord Cutler Beckett,

5
00:01:11,503 --> 00:01:14,631
duly appointed representative
of His Majesty, the king.

6
00:01:15,874 --> 00:01:18,775
By decree, according to martial law,

7
00:01:18,844 --> 00:01:22,302
the following statutes
are temporarily amended:

8
00:01:22,381 --> 00:01:25,077
Right to assembly, suspended.

9
00:01:27,919 --> 00:01:31,013
Right to habeas corpus, suspended.

10
00:01:32,591 --> 00:01:35,685
Right to legal counsel, suspended.

11
00:01:36,728 --> 00:01:40,960
Right to verdict by a jury of peers,
suspended.

12
00:01:42,367 --> 00:01:47,327
By decree, all persons
found guilty of piracy,

13
00:01:47,406 --> 00:01:50,534
or aiding a person convicted of piracy,

14
00:01:50,609 --> 00:01:54,545
or associating with a person
convicted of piracy...

15
00:01:55,614 --> 00:01:59,345
...shall be sentenced to
hang by the neck until dead."

16
00:02:32,116 --> 00:02:35,745
 The king and his men

17
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:40,484
 Stole the queen from her bed

18
00:02:42,193 --> 00:02:48,154
 And bound her in her bones

19
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:52,465
 The seas be ours

20
00:02:52,804 --> 00:02:56,865
 And by the powers

21
00:02:59,377 --> 00:03:05,111
 Where we will, we'll roam

22
00:03:12,290 --> 00:03:15,453
 Yo ho

23
00:03:16,094 --> 00:03:19,393
 All hands

24
00:03:19,831 --> 00:03:25,428
 Hoist the colors high

25
00:03:27,238 --> 00:03:30,469
[all] * Heave-ho

26
00:03:31,109 --> 00:03:35,011
 Thieves and beggars

27
00:03:35,079 --> 00:03:41,075
 Never shall we die

28
00:03:42,787 --> 00:03:46,245
 Yo ho

29
00:03:46,324 --> 00:03:50,055
 Haul together

30
00:03:50,128 --> 00:03:52,255
 Hoist the colors high

31
00:03:52,330 --> 00:03:53,490
Lord Beckett!

32
00:03:55,133 --> 00:03:57,761
They've started to sing, sir.

33
00:04:00,305 --> 00:04:04,605
- Finally.
- * Thieves and beggars

34
00:04:05,109 --> 00:04:10,945
 Never shall we die *

35
00:04:41,412 --> 00:04:45,576
[woman] * Some have died
and some are alive

36
00:04:46,517 --> 00:04:49,953
 Others sail on the sea

37
00:04:51,522 --> 00:04:53,956
 With the keys to the cage

38
00:04:54,125 --> 00:04:56,389
 And the devil to pay

39
00:04:56,527 --> 00:05:00,520
 We lay to Fiddler's Green

40
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:07,560
 The bell has been raised
from its watery grave

41
00:05:07,972 --> 00:05:11,305
 Hear its sepulchral tone

42
00:05:12,543 --> 00:05:17,537
 A call to all
Pay heed the squall

43
00:05:23,354 --> 00:05:25,914
[fireworks ignite]

44
00:05:25,990 --> 00:05:29,426
 Turn your sails to home

45
00:05:31,562 --> 00:05:33,587
 Yo ho

46
00:05:34,298 --> 00:05:36,266
 Haul together

47
00:05:36,634 --> 00:05:39,933
 Hoist the colors high

48
00:05:41,439 --> 00:05:44,067
 Heave-ho

49
00:05:44,142 --> 00:05:46,303
- * Thieve...
- Thief and beggar.

50
00:05:46,377 --> 00:05:49,676
 Never shall we die *

51
00:05:51,916 --> 00:05:55,079
A dangerous song to be singing...

52
00:05:55,286 --> 00:05:58,016
...for any who are
ignorant of its meaning.

53
00:05:58,623 --> 00:06:00,523
Particularly a woman.

54
00:06:02,093 --> 00:06:04,459
Particularly a woman alone.

55
00:06:05,229 --> 00:06:07,424
[man] What makes you think
she's alone?

56
00:06:12,036 --> 00:06:13,526
You protect her?

57
00:06:14,505 --> 00:06:16,905
And what makes you think
I need protecting?

58
00:06:17,308 --> 00:06:19,606
Your master's expecting us.

59
00:06:19,677 --> 00:06:24,046
And an unexpected death'd
cast a slight pall on our meeting.

60
00:06:25,650 --> 00:06:27,083
[man 2] Pick those feet up!

61
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:30,749
On the double!

62
00:06:32,156 --> 00:06:33,987
Eyes front!

63
00:06:42,834 --> 00:06:44,461
[man 2] No, no, no. None.

64
00:06:45,703 --> 00:06:47,694
[footsteps marching]

65
00:07:12,597 --> 00:07:15,293
[man] It always takes me
about a week to get my sea legs.

66
00:07:15,366 --> 00:07:17,527
I was throwing up horribly
the last time.

67
00:07:28,880 --> 00:07:31,940
- [squawk] Steady as she goes.
- [hurdy-gurdy music plays]

68
00:07:34,318 --> 00:07:35,307
All right.

69
00:07:39,023 --> 00:07:40,923
Have you heard anything from Will?

70
00:07:40,992 --> 00:07:43,324
I trust young Turner
to acquire the charts

71
00:07:43,394 --> 00:07:46,830
and you to remember your place
in the presence of Captain Sao Feng.

72
00:07:47,365 --> 00:07:49,424
Is he that terrifying?

73
00:07:49,500 --> 00:07:54,437
He's much like myself, but absent my
merciful nature and sense of fair play.

74
00:07:59,043 --> 00:08:00,101
[speaks in Chinese]

75
00:08:09,453 --> 00:08:12,047
- [metal cracks]
- We're through. Make ready.

76
00:08:12,123 --> 00:08:13,715
[creaking]

77
00:08:18,563 --> 00:08:19,757
Hurry now.

78
00:08:26,170 --> 00:08:30,038
Do you think because she is a woman
we would not suspect her of treachery?

79
00:08:32,376 --> 00:08:34,401
Well, when you put it that way...

80
00:08:35,079 --> 00:08:36,876
Remove. Please.

81
00:09:21,726 --> 00:09:24,752
Remove. Please.

82
00:10:10,007 --> 00:10:14,273
Captain Barbossa,
welcome to Singapore.

83
00:10:17,114 --> 00:10:18,775
[sniffles]

84
00:10:18,849 --> 00:10:19,975
More steam.

85
00:10:23,587 --> 00:10:26,579
- [hissing]
- [men shouting in Chinese]

86
00:10:38,469 --> 00:10:40,801
[grunting]

87
00:10:42,540 --> 00:10:43,529
None of that.

88
00:10:43,607 --> 00:10:47,566
If things don't go the way we want,
then we're the only chance they've got.

89
00:10:53,851 --> 00:10:58,447
I understand that you have
a request to make of me.

90
00:10:58,522 --> 00:11:01,582
More of a proposal to put to ye.

91
00:11:01,992 --> 00:11:07,396
I've a venture underway and I find
myself in need of a ship and a crew.

92
00:11:08,432 --> 00:11:12,232
Hmm. It's an odd coincidence.

93
00:11:12,303 --> 00:11:15,397
Because you happen to have
a ship and a crew you don't need?

94
00:11:15,473 --> 00:11:17,873
No.

95
00:11:17,942 --> 00:11:20,570
Because earlier this day,
not far from here,

96
00:11:20,644 --> 00:11:24,603
a thief broke into
my most revered uncle's temple

97
00:11:24,749 --> 00:11:27,775
and tried to make off with these.

98
00:11:29,420 --> 00:11:32,116
The navigational charts.

99
00:11:32,757 --> 00:11:36,488
The route to the Farthest Gate.

100
00:11:38,996 --> 00:11:43,456
Wouldn't it be amazing
if this venture of yours

101
00:11:43,534 --> 00:11:46,935
took you to the world beyond this one?

102
00:11:47,004 --> 00:11:49,871
It would strain credulity at that.

103
00:11:57,281 --> 00:11:59,545
[splashing]

104
00:11:59,617 --> 00:12:01,744
[gasping]

105
00:12:03,921 --> 00:12:08,620
This is the thief.
Is his face familiar to you?

106
00:12:11,429 --> 00:12:14,865
Then I guess he has
no further need for it.

107
00:12:15,199 --> 00:12:16,188
No, no!

108
00:12:25,109 --> 00:12:30,012
You come into my city
and you betray my hospitality.

109
00:12:30,881 --> 00:12:34,373
- I assure you I had no idea...
- That he would get caught!

110
00:12:38,789 --> 00:12:41,952
You intend to attempt the voyage
to Davy Jones' Locker.

111
00:12:42,026 --> 00:12:44,358
But I cannot help but wonder...

112
00:12:46,030 --> 00:12:47,395
Why?

113
00:12:56,841 --> 00:13:02,575
The song has been sung.
The time is upon us.

114
00:13:03,080 --> 00:13:06,846
We must convene the Brethren Court.

115
00:13:06,917 --> 00:13:12,878
As one of the nine pirate lords,
you must honor the call.

116
00:13:14,758 --> 00:13:15,918
More steam.

117
00:13:22,900 --> 00:13:24,424
More steam!

118
00:13:26,937 --> 00:13:28,165
[groans]

119
00:13:31,542 --> 00:13:35,034
- There is a price on all our heads.
- Aye.

120
00:13:35,112 --> 00:13:37,012
It is true.

121
00:13:37,081 --> 00:13:40,346
It seems the only way a pirate
can turn a profit anymore...

122
00:13:41,986 --> 00:13:45,387
...is by betraying other pirates.

123
00:13:45,456 --> 00:13:47,822
[Barbossa] We must put
our differences aside.

124
00:13:47,892 --> 00:13:48,950
Wait for the signal.

125
00:13:49,026 --> 00:13:52,189
[Barbossa] The First Brethren Court
gave us rule of the seas.

126
00:13:52,263 --> 00:13:56,927
But now that rule is being challenged
by Lord Cutler Beckett.

127
00:13:57,067 --> 00:13:59,797
[Sao Feng] Against East India
Trading Company,

128
00:13:59,870 --> 00:14:02,532
what value is the Brethren Court?

129
00:14:03,207 --> 00:14:05,038
What can any of us do?

130
00:14:05,109 --> 00:14:07,942
You can fight!
Get off me!

131
00:14:08,012 --> 00:14:11,470
You are Sao Feng,
the pirate lord of Singapore.

132
00:14:11,549 --> 00:14:16,248
You command in an age of piracy
where bold captains sail free waters.

133
00:14:16,754 --> 00:14:20,190
Where waves aren't measured in feet,
but as increments of fear,

134
00:14:20,257 --> 00:14:22,521
and those who pass the test
become legend.

135
00:14:23,160 --> 00:14:25,958
Would you have that era
come to an end on your watch?

136
00:14:26,497 --> 00:14:29,159
The most notorious pirates
from around the world

137
00:14:29,233 --> 00:14:31,224
are uniting against our enemy,

138
00:14:31,302 --> 00:14:34,396
and yet you sit here
cowering in your bathwater!

139
00:14:39,843 --> 00:14:41,743
Elizabeth Swann.

140
00:14:42,279 --> 00:14:45,976
There is more to you
than meets the eye, isn't there?

141
00:14:46,750 --> 00:14:50,709
And the eye does not go wanting.

142
00:14:52,256 --> 00:14:57,626
But I cannot help but notice
you have failed to answer my question.

143
00:14:58,963 --> 00:15:02,364
What is it you seek
in Davy Jones' Locker?

144
00:15:02,433 --> 00:15:03,627
Jack Sparrow.

145
00:15:04,802 --> 00:15:06,599
[giggling]

146
00:15:06,670 --> 00:15:08,729
He's one of the pirate lords.

147
00:15:14,612 --> 00:15:16,102
[Sao Feng] The only reason...

148
00:15:17,114 --> 00:15:21,141
...I would want Jack Sparrow returned
from the land of the dead...

149
00:15:21,852 --> 00:15:25,185
...is so I can send him back myself!

150
00:15:29,660 --> 00:15:33,892
Jack Sparrow holds
one of the nine pieces of eight.

151
00:15:33,964 --> 00:15:37,422
He failed to pass it along
to a successor before he died.

152
00:15:37,501 --> 00:15:40,129
So we must go and get him back.

153
00:15:45,342 --> 00:15:48,675
So you admit...

154
00:15:49,013 --> 00:15:51,277
...you have deceived me.

155
00:15:51,348 --> 00:15:52,747
Weapons!

156
00:15:52,816 --> 00:15:54,647
[yelling]

157
00:15:55,953 --> 00:15:57,147
Weapons.

158
00:15:57,821 --> 00:16:01,814
Sao Feng, I assure you,
our intentions are strictly honorable.

159
00:16:08,932 --> 00:16:11,833
Drop your weapons or I kill the man!

160
00:16:14,271 --> 00:16:16,068
Kill him. He's not our man.

161
00:16:18,142 --> 00:16:21,043
If he's not with you,
and he's not with us...

162
00:16:21,712 --> 00:16:23,612
...who's he with?

163
00:16:23,681 --> 00:16:26,081
- [door opens]
- Charge!

164
00:16:46,937 --> 00:16:47,926
Will!

165
00:16:55,813 --> 00:16:56,802
[gasps]

166
00:17:02,319 --> 00:17:04,844
[man] Look out! Look behind you!

167
00:17:04,922 --> 00:17:07,117
[clamoring]

168
00:17:07,825 --> 00:17:09,087
Ready!

169
00:17:10,661 --> 00:17:11,650
Fire.

170
00:17:14,331 --> 00:17:15,992
[shouts]

171
00:17:21,472 --> 00:17:22,461
Chart!

172
00:17:48,132 --> 00:17:49,156
Boo!

173
00:17:53,504 --> 00:17:58,134
Company! Make ready. Set. Fire!

174
00:18:05,115 --> 00:18:06,104
Company!

175
00:18:06,183 --> 00:18:08,276
Make ready. Set.

176
00:18:08,352 --> 00:18:11,150
[hurdy-gurdy music playing]

177
00:18:11,221 --> 00:18:13,416
- Fire!
- [explosion]

178
00:18:15,292 --> 00:18:17,954
[shouting and gunfire continues]

179
00:18:23,934 --> 00:18:25,265
[metal clanging on floor]

180
00:18:29,239 --> 00:18:31,673
It's an odd coincidence, isn't it?

181
00:18:31,742 --> 00:18:36,679
The East India Trading Company finds me
the day you show up in Singapore.

182
00:18:36,747 --> 00:18:38,180
It is coincidence only.

183
00:18:39,349 --> 00:18:40,816
[gun cocks]

184
00:18:41,919 --> 00:18:45,787
If you want to make a deal with Beckett,
you need what I offer.

185
00:18:47,024 --> 00:18:51,518
You cross Barbossa.
You are willing to cross Jack Sparrow.

186
00:18:51,962 --> 00:18:54,624
Why should I expect any better?

187
00:18:54,698 --> 00:18:57,565
I need the Black Pearl
to free my father.

188
00:19:00,838 --> 00:19:02,465
You're helping me to get it.

189
00:19:11,615 --> 00:19:13,048
[squawks] Uh-oh.

190
00:19:18,388 --> 00:19:19,855
Fire in the hole.

191
00:19:27,798 --> 00:19:29,527
[popping and exploding]

192
00:19:34,538 --> 00:19:36,267
[man screaming]

193
00:19:38,041 --> 00:19:39,235
Thank you, Jack.

194
00:19:39,309 --> 00:19:41,402
- [screeches]
- [squawk] Thank you, Jack.

195
00:19:43,647 --> 00:19:46,445
- You have the charts?
- And better yet.

196
00:19:47,084 --> 00:19:49,848
- A ship and a crew.
- Where's Sao Feng?

197
00:19:49,920 --> 00:19:52,753
He'll cover our escape
and meet us at Shipwreck Cove.

198
00:19:52,823 --> 00:19:54,654
This way. Be quick.

199
00:20:00,998 --> 00:20:03,694
[Gibbs] Haul the snotters with a will!

200
00:20:03,767 --> 00:20:05,462
[Pintel] Keep it taut.

201
00:20:06,536 --> 00:20:09,528
[Gibbs] Secure provisions
and stow the cargo.

202
00:20:09,606 --> 00:20:12,439
There's no place left
for Sao Feng to cower.

203
00:20:13,043 --> 00:20:15,409
Do you think he will honor the call?

204
00:20:15,979 --> 00:20:17,947
I cannot say.

205
00:20:18,815 --> 00:20:21,079
There's an evil on these seas

206
00:20:21,151 --> 00:20:26,487
that even the most staunch and
bloodthirsty pirates have come to fear.

207
00:20:56,153 --> 00:20:58,144
[cannon fire booming]

208
00:21:33,590 --> 00:21:35,057
A piece of eight.

209
00:21:35,792 --> 00:21:37,521
Nine of them, you say.

210
00:21:37,594 --> 00:21:40,688
Our new friend in Singapore
was very specific, sir.

211
00:21:40,764 --> 00:21:42,356
Nine pieces of eight.

212
00:21:42,432 --> 00:21:44,992
What's the significance of that,
I wonder.

213
00:21:45,068 --> 00:21:47,400
Nothing can hold against the armada.

214
00:21:47,471 --> 00:21:49,701
Not with the Flying Dutchman
at the lead.

215
00:21:49,773 --> 00:21:51,297
Nothing we know of.

216
00:21:52,676 --> 00:21:56,009
Did your friend mention
where the Brethren Court are meeting?

217
00:21:56,079 --> 00:21:58,809
He was mum on that, sir.

218
00:21:58,882 --> 00:22:00,349
Hmm.

219
00:22:00,417 --> 00:22:02,851
Then he knows the value of information.

220
00:22:02,919 --> 00:22:05,717
Better keep this between ourselves.

221
00:22:05,789 --> 00:22:09,088
We don't want anyone
running off to Singapore, do we?

222
00:22:10,961 --> 00:22:15,694
- Ah, admiral.
- You summoned me, Lord Beckett.

223
00:22:15,766 --> 00:22:18,496
Yes. Something for you there.

224
00:22:18,568 --> 00:22:21,935
Your new station deserves an old friend.

225
00:22:35,719 --> 00:22:39,052
- Not more requisition orders.
- No, sir.

226
00:22:39,122 --> 00:22:40,680
Execution.

227
00:22:40,757 --> 00:22:42,349
[sword unsheathed]

228
00:22:46,563 --> 00:22:48,758
The Brethren know they face extinction.

229
00:22:49,766 --> 00:22:54,135
All that remains is for them to decide
where they make their final stand.

230
00:23:18,929 --> 00:23:22,126
No one said anything about cold.

231
00:23:22,199 --> 00:23:25,965
I'm sure there must be
a good reason for our suffering.

232
00:23:26,370 --> 00:23:29,305
Why don't that obeah woman
bring Jack back the same way

233
00:23:29,373 --> 00:23:33,901
- she brought back Barbossa?
- Because Barbossa was only dead.

234
00:23:35,212 --> 00:23:39,478
Jack Sparrow is taken, body and soul,

235
00:23:39,550 --> 00:23:44,715
to a place not of death, but punishment.

236
00:23:44,788 --> 00:23:48,622
The worst fate a person
can bring upon himself...

237
00:23:49,059 --> 00:23:51,289
...stretching on forever.

238
00:23:51,729 --> 00:23:55,221
That's what awaits
at Davy Jones' Locker.

239
00:23:59,403 --> 00:24:02,770
Yeah. I knew there was a good reason.

240
00:24:10,214 --> 00:24:12,205
[groaning]

241
00:24:13,751 --> 00:24:16,015
[shudders] Nothing here is set.

242
00:24:17,221 --> 00:24:19,382
These can't be
as accurate as modern charts.

243
00:24:19,456 --> 00:24:24,120
No. But it leads to more places.

244
00:24:30,200 --> 00:24:31,724
"Over the edge.

245
00:24:31,802 --> 00:24:34,236
Over again."

246
00:24:36,073 --> 00:24:38,769
"Sunrise sets.

247
00:24:38,842 --> 00:24:41,834
Flash of green."

248
00:24:42,713 --> 00:24:46,706
Do you care to interpret,
Captain Barbossa?

249
00:24:47,518 --> 00:24:52,387
Ever gazed upon the green flash,
Master Gibbs?

250
00:24:52,456 --> 00:24:57,018
I reckon I seen my fair share.
Happens on rare occasion.

251
00:24:57,094 --> 00:25:00,894
The last glimpse of sunset,
a green flash shoots up into the sky.

252
00:25:01,298 --> 00:25:04,290
Some go their whole lives
without ever seeing it.

253
00:25:04,368 --> 00:25:07,201
Some claim to have seen it who ain't.
And some say...

254
00:25:07,271 --> 00:25:11,435
It signals when a soul
comes back to this world from the dead.

255
00:25:16,013 --> 00:25:19,608
- Sorry.
- Trust me, young Master Turner.

256
00:25:19,683 --> 00:25:23,016
It's not getting to the land
of the dead that's the problem.

257
00:25:25,255 --> 00:25:27,018
It's getting back.

258
00:26:07,564 --> 00:26:07,665
[man] Bosun!
Head to wind! Lift the sail!

259
00:26:07,665 --> 00:26:12,261
[man] Bosun!
Head to wind! Lift the sail!

260
00:26:12,336 --> 00:26:13,564
[man 2] Aye, aye, sir!

261
00:26:15,739 --> 00:26:18,833
Bloody hell. There's nothing left.

262
00:26:18,909 --> 00:26:22,276
Jones is a loose cannon, sir.

263
00:26:22,746 --> 00:26:24,714
Fetch the chest.

264
00:26:25,416 --> 00:26:27,577
And the governor?

265
00:26:28,352 --> 00:26:32,118
He's been asking questions
about the heart.

266
00:26:32,690 --> 00:26:34,521
Does he know?

267
00:26:38,095 --> 00:26:41,758
Then perhaps his usefulness
has run its course.

268
00:26:51,942 --> 00:26:55,343
- [organ playing]
- [sighing]

269
00:27:01,719 --> 00:27:04,711
[music box playing]

270
00:27:26,877 --> 00:27:28,208
[music box stops]

271
00:27:41,992 --> 00:27:44,017
[man] Step lively, men.

272
00:27:44,461 --> 00:27:46,019
Step lively!

273
00:27:48,098 --> 00:27:49,292
Stand fast!

274
00:27:56,006 --> 00:27:57,701
Steady, men.

275
00:28:03,480 --> 00:28:09,350
Go. All of you.
And take that infernal thing with you.

276
00:28:09,419 --> 00:28:12,388
I will not have it on my ship!

277
00:28:12,456 --> 00:28:15,983
Oh, I'm sorry to hear that...
because I will.

278
00:28:16,059 --> 00:28:18,960
Because it seems to be
the only way to ensure

279
00:28:19,029 --> 00:28:22,430
that this ship do as directed
by the company.

280
00:28:27,638 --> 00:28:30,072
We need prisoners to interrogate,

281
00:28:30,140 --> 00:28:33,337
which tends to work best
when they're alive.

282
00:28:33,410 --> 00:28:37,312
The Dutchman sails
as its captain commands.

283
00:28:37,381 --> 00:28:41,283
And its captain
is to sail it as commanded.

284
00:28:43,887 --> 00:28:47,983
I would have thought you'd learned that
when I ordered you to kill your pet.

285
00:28:48,959 --> 00:28:52,656
This is no longer your world, Jones.

286
00:28:53,497 --> 00:28:56,864
The immaterial has become...

287
00:28:58,001 --> 00:28:59,730
...immaterial.

288
00:29:00,838 --> 00:29:02,931
[Norrington] Charge your bayonets.

289
00:29:03,907 --> 00:29:06,842
[heartbeat]

290
00:29:41,912 --> 00:29:44,312
How long do we continue not talking?

291
00:29:48,318 --> 00:29:50,843
Once we rescue Jack,
everything'll be fine.

292
00:29:53,957 --> 00:29:55,390
Then we rescue Jack.

293
00:30:20,684 --> 00:30:23,881
For what we want most...

294
00:30:25,889 --> 00:30:29,188
...there is a cost
must be paid in the end.

295
00:30:40,470 --> 00:30:45,407
- Barbossa, ahead!
- Aye, we're good and lost now.

296
00:30:46,310 --> 00:30:48,938
- Lost?
- For certain you have to be lost

297
00:30:49,012 --> 00:30:51,003
to find a place as can't be found.

298
00:30:51,081 --> 00:30:53,447
Elseways, everyone
would know where it was.

299
00:30:53,717 --> 00:30:56,185
- We're gaining speed.
- Aye.

300
00:30:57,187 --> 00:30:58,779
To stations!

301
00:30:59,890 --> 00:31:01,289
All hands to stations!

302
00:31:01,358 --> 00:31:04,122
- [men shouting]
- Huh?

303
00:31:04,194 --> 00:31:07,095
Rudder full. Hard aport! Gather way.

304
00:31:07,164 --> 00:31:11,999
Nay! Belay that!
Let her run straight and true!

305
00:31:13,904 --> 00:31:15,132
Blimey.

306
00:31:29,152 --> 00:31:32,519
- You've doomed us all.
- Don't be so unkind.

307
00:31:32,589 --> 00:31:35,251
You may not survive
to pass this way again,

308
00:31:35,325 --> 00:31:39,159
and these be the last
friendly words you'll hear.

309
00:31:43,934 --> 00:31:45,162
Tie her off.

310
00:31:45,669 --> 00:31:48,832
[muttering]

311
00:31:50,841 --> 00:31:52,536
Hard aport.

312
00:31:59,249 --> 00:32:01,217
[men shouting]

313
00:32:01,284 --> 00:32:03,309
Hold on!

314
00:32:31,314 --> 00:32:35,182
- [music playing]
- [cannons firing]

315
00:32:35,252 --> 00:32:39,882
[man laughs] Strike your colors,
you blooming cockroaches.

316
00:32:39,956 --> 00:32:45,485
[man 2] Dead men tell no tales.

317
00:32:45,562 --> 00:32:47,393
[echoing]

318
00:32:49,933 --> 00:32:51,992
[man sniffing]

319
00:33:18,962 --> 00:33:20,691
[gunshot]

320
00:33:33,677 --> 00:33:35,110
My peanut.

321
00:33:36,713 --> 00:33:38,772
Haul the halyard. Slacken braces.

322
00:33:38,849 --> 00:33:41,147
Aye, captain.
Slacken braces.

323
00:33:41,218 --> 00:33:43,345
Step lively with a will.

324
00:33:43,420 --> 00:33:45,012
- Aye, aye.
- Right away, sir.

325
00:33:45,088 --> 00:33:46,783
I want a fire down below!

326
00:33:49,893 --> 00:33:53,624
- Help.
- Man the yards, you filthy toads.

327
00:33:53,697 --> 00:33:56,029
[muffled groaning]

328
00:33:56,867 --> 00:33:58,596
[clucking]

329
00:34:04,608 --> 00:34:08,408
[all] Haul! Haul!

330
00:34:08,478 --> 00:34:11,345
Haul the sheets handsomely.
Scandalize the lateens.

331
00:34:11,414 --> 00:34:13,211
- Aye, sir.
- Right away, sir.

332
00:34:27,230 --> 00:34:29,198
- Mr. Sparrow?
- Aye, cap'n.

333
00:34:29,966 --> 00:34:32,696
What say you about
the condition of this tack line?

334
00:34:33,403 --> 00:34:36,839
- It be proper to my eyes, sir.
- Proper?

335
00:34:36,907 --> 00:34:40,775
It is neither proper nor suitable, sir.
It is not acceptable nor adequate.

336
00:34:40,844 --> 00:34:43,278
It is, in obvious fact, an abomination.

337
00:34:43,346 --> 00:34:46,873
Begging your pardon, sir,
but if you gave a man another chance.

338
00:34:47,284 --> 00:34:49,218
- Shall I?
- [unsheathes sword]

339
00:34:53,190 --> 00:34:56,250
It's that sort of thinking
got us into this mess.

340
00:34:58,995 --> 00:35:01,862
We have lost speed and, therefore, time.

341
00:35:01,932 --> 00:35:06,164
Precious time,
which cannot be recovered once lost.

342
00:35:06,236 --> 00:35:08,033
Do you understand?

343
00:35:08,104 --> 00:35:11,232
- [all] Aye, aye, captain.
- Do you now?

344
00:35:11,775 --> 00:35:14,972
It will all have to be redone.
All of it.

345
00:35:15,045 --> 00:35:17,513
And let this serve
as a lesson to the lot of you.

346
00:35:17,581 --> 00:35:21,073
Doldrums, sir,
has the entire crew on edge.

347
00:35:21,151 --> 00:35:24,120
I have no sympathy
for any of you feculent maggots,

348
00:35:24,187 --> 00:35:27,088
and no more patience
to pretend otherwise.

349
00:35:29,893 --> 00:35:33,886
Gentlemen, I wash my hands
of this weirdness.

350
00:35:44,441 --> 00:35:45,840
No wind.

351
00:35:46,343 --> 00:35:48,311
Of course, there's no bloody wind.

352
00:35:49,946 --> 00:35:52,414
My soul I do swear for a breeze.

353
00:35:52,482 --> 00:35:57,317
Gust. Whisper. A tiny, miniature lick.

354
00:36:10,100 --> 00:36:12,591
Yes, sir, I know, but why?
Why would he do that?

355
00:36:12,669 --> 00:36:14,364
Because he's a lummox, isn't he?

356
00:36:14,437 --> 00:36:18,134
We shall have a magnificent
garden party and you're not invited.

357
00:36:31,388 --> 00:36:32,821
Shoo.

358
00:36:51,841 --> 00:36:53,103
A rock.

359
00:37:20,403 --> 00:37:23,964
Now we're being followed by rocks.
Never had that before.

360
00:37:24,274 --> 00:37:25,263
Oh.

361
00:37:26,743 --> 00:37:28,301
A rope.

362
00:37:34,284 --> 00:37:37,276
[grunting]

363
00:37:49,699 --> 00:37:51,690
[panting]

364
00:39:09,045 --> 00:39:11,707
[rumbling]

365
00:39:41,811 --> 00:39:43,676
[coughing and gagging]

366
00:39:49,018 --> 00:39:52,112
This truly is a godforsaken place.

367
00:39:52,889 --> 00:39:55,949
I don't see Jack.
I don't see anyone.

368
00:39:57,293 --> 00:39:58,920
He's here.

369
00:39:58,995 --> 00:40:02,294
Davy Jones never once
gave up that what he took.

370
00:40:02,365 --> 00:40:03,798
And does it matter?

371
00:40:03,867 --> 00:40:06,927
We're trapped here by your doing.
No different than Jack.

372
00:40:10,106 --> 00:40:15,271
Witty Jack is closer than you think.

373
00:40:55,885 --> 00:40:57,443
Impossible.

374
00:41:03,092 --> 00:41:04,081
Boat.

375
00:41:10,466 --> 00:41:14,368
Slap me thrice
and hand me to me mama. It's Jack.

376
00:41:23,513 --> 00:41:26,209
- It's the captain!
- A sight for sore eyes.

377
00:41:26,282 --> 00:41:27,374
[parrot] Hide the rum.

378
00:41:27,450 --> 00:41:29,384
- Mr. Gibbs.
- Aye, cap'n.

379
00:41:29,452 --> 00:41:30,817
I thought so.

380
00:41:30,887 --> 00:41:33,856
I expect you're able
to account for your actions, then.

381
00:41:33,923 --> 00:41:34,912
Sir?

382
00:41:34,991 --> 00:41:39,018
There's been a perpetual and virulent
lack of discipline upon my vessel. Why?

383
00:41:39,095 --> 00:41:41,427
- Why is that, sir?
- Sir, you're...

384
00:41:42,265 --> 00:41:45,063
You're in Davy Jones' Locker, cap'n.

385
00:41:48,771 --> 00:41:52,070
I know that.
I know where I am.

386
00:41:52,141 --> 00:41:56,373
- And don't think I don't.
- Jack Sparrow.

387
00:41:57,080 --> 00:41:58,945
Oh, Hector.

388
00:41:59,015 --> 00:42:00,949
It's been too long. Hasn't it?

389
00:42:01,017 --> 00:42:05,818
Aye. Isla de Muerta, remember?
You shot me.

390
00:42:07,223 --> 00:42:09,088
No, I didn't.

391
00:42:09,158 --> 00:42:11,251
Tia Dalma, out and about, eh?

392
00:42:11,327 --> 00:42:15,229
You add an agreeable sense
of the macabre to any delirium.

393
00:42:16,566 --> 00:42:18,466
He thinks we're a hallucination.

394
00:42:19,369 --> 00:42:21,030
William, tell me something:

395
00:42:21,104 --> 00:42:25,131
Have you come because you need my help
to save a certain distressing damsel,

396
00:42:25,208 --> 00:42:28,769
or rather, damsel in distress?
Either one.

397
00:42:29,312 --> 00:42:30,301
No.

398
00:42:30,380 --> 00:42:32,814
Well, then,
you wouldn't be here, would you?

399
00:42:32,882 --> 00:42:36,875
So you can't be here.
Q.E.D., you're not really here.

400
00:42:36,953 --> 00:42:38,318
[Elizabeth] Jack.

401
00:42:40,490 --> 00:42:42,424
This is real. We're here.

402
00:42:53,403 --> 00:42:56,065
- The Locker, you say?
- Aye.

403
00:42:56,506 --> 00:42:58,804
We've come to rescue you.

404
00:43:00,410 --> 00:43:03,937
Have you, now?
That's very kind of you.

405
00:43:04,013 --> 00:43:07,346
But it would seem that
as I possess a ship and you don't,

406
00:43:07,483 --> 00:43:11,579
you're the ones in need of rescuing,
and I'm not sure as I'm in the mood.

407
00:43:12,422 --> 00:43:15,949
I see my ship. Right there.

408
00:43:19,462 --> 00:43:20,986
Can't spot it.

409
00:43:21,064 --> 00:43:24,397
Must be a tiny little thing
hiding somewhere behind the Pearl.

410
00:43:24,467 --> 00:43:27,265
Jack, Cutler Beckett
has the heart of Davy Jones.

411
00:43:27,336 --> 00:43:30,703
- He controls the Flying Dutchman.
- He's taking over the seas.

412
00:43:30,773 --> 00:43:33,640
The song has been sung.
The Brethren Court is called.

413
00:43:33,710 --> 00:43:37,510
Leave you alone for a minute, look
what happens. Everything's gone to pot.

414
00:43:37,580 --> 00:43:40,242
Aye. The world
needs you back something fierce.

415
00:43:40,316 --> 00:43:42,113
And you need a crew.

416
00:43:44,220 --> 00:43:46,415
Why should I sail with any of you?

417
00:43:46,489 --> 00:43:49,981
Four of you have tried to kill me
in the past. One of you succeeded.

418
00:43:53,730 --> 00:43:57,166
Oh, she's not told you.

419
00:43:57,233 --> 00:44:00,066
You'll have loads to talk about
while you're here.

420
00:44:01,437 --> 00:44:02,904
As for you...

421
00:44:04,140 --> 00:44:10,010
Now... don't tell me
you didn't enjoy it at the time.

422
00:44:10,980 --> 00:44:13,039
Fair enough. All right, you're in.

423
00:44:13,116 --> 00:44:15,050
Don't need you. You scare me.

424
00:44:15,118 --> 00:44:18,986
Gibbs, you can come. Marty. Mm...

425
00:44:19,055 --> 00:44:20,716
- Cotton.
- [parrot squawks]

426
00:44:20,790 --> 00:44:25,318
Cotton's parrot, I'm a little iffy, but
at least I'll have someone to talk to.

427
00:44:26,496 --> 00:44:27,793
Who are you?

428
00:44:27,864 --> 00:44:30,162
Tai Huang. These are my men.

429
00:44:30,833 --> 00:44:34,667
- Where do your allegiances lie?
- With the highest bidder.

430
00:44:35,104 --> 00:44:38,767
- I have a ship.
- That makes you the highest bidder.

431
00:44:38,841 --> 00:44:42,868
Good man. Weigh anchor, all hands.
Prepare to make sail.

432
00:44:42,945 --> 00:44:44,139
[parrot] Weigh anchor.

433
00:44:47,717 --> 00:44:49,309
[Barbossa] Jack...

434
00:44:50,653 --> 00:44:54,248
Which way are you going, Jack?

435
00:44:56,392 --> 00:44:59,691
Trim that sail.
Slack windward brace and sheet.

436
00:44:59,762 --> 00:45:02,322
- Haul the pennant line.
- Haul the pennant line.

437
00:45:02,398 --> 00:45:03,831
What are you doing?

438
00:45:03,900 --> 00:45:06,494
- What are you doing?
- No, what are you doing?

439
00:45:06,569 --> 00:45:09,333
- What are you doing?
- No, what are you doing?

440
00:45:09,405 --> 00:45:11,896
What are you?
Captain gives orders on the ship.

441
00:45:11,974 --> 00:45:14,135
The captain of the ship
is giving orders.

442
00:45:14,710 --> 00:45:17,702
- My ship, makes me captain.
- They be my charts.

443
00:45:17,780 --> 00:45:20,271
That makes you "chartman."

444
00:45:20,349 --> 00:45:25,252
Stow it! The both of you!
That's an order! Understand?!

445
00:45:31,093 --> 00:45:34,290
Sorry. I just thought
with the captain issue in doubt,

446
00:45:34,363 --> 00:45:37,298
I'd throw in my name for consideration.

447
00:45:37,366 --> 00:45:38,628
Sorry.

448
00:45:43,372 --> 00:45:45,670
- I'd vote for you.
- Yeah?

449
00:46:21,077 --> 00:46:22,942
You left Jack to the Kraken.

450
00:46:24,013 --> 00:46:26,140
He's rescued now. It's done with.

451
00:46:29,318 --> 00:46:31,286
Will, I had no choice.

452
00:46:32,521 --> 00:46:34,386
You chose not to tell me.

453
00:46:35,324 --> 00:46:37,053
I couldn't.

454
00:46:38,394 --> 00:46:39,986
It wasn't your burden to bear.

455
00:46:40,997 --> 00:46:42,862
But I did bear it, didn't I?

456
00:46:44,567 --> 00:46:46,432
I just didn't know what it was.

457
00:46:47,236 --> 00:46:48,863
I thought...

458
00:46:50,039 --> 00:46:52,030
You thought I loved him.

459
00:47:00,549 --> 00:47:04,383
If you make your choices alone,
how can I trust you?

460
00:47:07,390 --> 00:47:08,687
You can't.

461
00:47:27,143 --> 00:47:29,475
[ship creaking]

462
00:47:29,545 --> 00:47:31,342
[gasps]

463
00:47:37,253 --> 00:47:38,413
Eerie.

464
00:47:40,189 --> 00:47:42,350
That's downright macabre.

465
00:47:47,897 --> 00:47:51,560
I wonder what would happen if you
dropped a cannonball on one of them?

466
00:47:54,070 --> 00:47:56,231
[both chuckling]

467
00:48:01,477 --> 00:48:03,672
Be disrespectful, it would.

468
00:48:03,746 --> 00:48:07,512
They should be
in the care of Davy Jones.

469
00:48:09,151 --> 00:48:12,314
That was the duty
him was charged with...

470
00:48:12,621 --> 00:48:15,317
...by the goddess, Calypso.

471
00:48:17,193 --> 00:48:20,993
To ferry those who die at sea
to the other side.

472
00:48:24,300 --> 00:48:26,268
And every ten years...

473
00:48:27,069 --> 00:48:29,196
...him could come ashore...

474
00:48:30,239 --> 00:48:34,539
...to be with she
who love him... truly.

475
00:48:38,881 --> 00:48:41,475
But the man has become a monster.

476
00:48:42,084 --> 00:48:46,111
So he wasn't always... tentacley?

477
00:48:48,224 --> 00:48:49,384
No.

478
00:48:51,594 --> 00:48:53,994
Him was a man...

479
00:48:54,063 --> 00:48:56,054
...once.

480
00:49:03,372 --> 00:49:05,397
Now there's boats coming.

481
00:49:13,082 --> 00:49:15,846
They're not a threat to us. Am I right?

482
00:49:16,452 --> 00:49:19,046
We are nothing but ghosts to them.

483
00:49:19,121 --> 00:49:21,385
It's best just let them be.

484
00:49:35,871 --> 00:49:38,339
It's my father. We've made it back.

485
00:49:38,407 --> 00:49:40,967
Father! Father, here, look here!

486
00:49:42,778 --> 00:49:43,870
Elizabeth.

487
00:49:45,815 --> 00:49:47,339
We're not back.

488
00:49:54,457 --> 00:49:55,481
Father!

489
00:49:58,761 --> 00:50:02,253
Elizabeth. Are you dead?

490
00:50:03,499 --> 00:50:04,830
No. No.

491
00:50:05,835 --> 00:50:07,530
I think I am.

492
00:50:07,603 --> 00:50:09,594
No, you can't be.

493
00:50:10,739 --> 00:50:13,606
There was this chest, you see.

494
00:50:14,376 --> 00:50:18,039
It's odd.
At the time it seemed so important.

495
00:50:18,714 --> 00:50:19,942
Come aboard.

496
00:50:20,015 --> 00:50:21,778
And a heart.

497
00:50:21,851 --> 00:50:27,118
I learned that if you stab the heart,
yours must take its place.

498
00:50:27,189 --> 00:50:30,716
And you will sail the seas for eternity.

499
00:50:30,793 --> 00:50:33,785
The Dutchman must have a captain.

500
00:50:34,864 --> 00:50:36,855
Silly thing to die for.

501
00:50:37,233 --> 00:50:40,464
Someone, cast a line.
Come back with us!

502
00:50:42,304 --> 00:50:43,396
Come on.

503
00:50:44,473 --> 00:50:48,705
A touch... of destiny.

504
00:50:51,580 --> 00:50:52,877
Take the line.

505
00:50:55,384 --> 00:50:57,579
I'm so proud of you, Elizabeth.

506
00:50:57,653 --> 00:50:59,382
Father, the line! Take the line!

507
00:51:03,192 --> 00:51:05,524
- She must not leave the ship!
- Father!

508
00:51:05,594 --> 00:51:09,860
Please come with us!
Please! No! I won't leave you!

509
00:51:10,499 --> 00:51:12,490
I'll give your love to your mother.

510
00:51:12,568 --> 00:51:15,435
- Please, I won't let you go!
- Elizabeth.

511
00:51:15,504 --> 00:51:17,563
- Elizabeth.
- [sobbing]

512
00:51:23,679 --> 00:51:24,941
Is there a way?

513
00:51:29,752 --> 00:51:31,447
Him at peace.

514
00:51:38,861 --> 00:51:40,419
No water.

515
00:51:41,564 --> 00:51:43,657
Why is all but the rum gone?

516
00:51:45,301 --> 00:51:46,734
Rum's gone too.

517
00:51:54,910 --> 00:51:59,540
If we cannot escape
these doldrums before night,

518
00:51:59,615 --> 00:52:03,517
I fear we will sail on trackless seas.

519
00:52:03,586 --> 00:52:06,817
Doomed to roam the reach between worlds.

520
00:52:07,623 --> 00:52:08,715
Forever.

521
00:52:08,791 --> 00:52:12,750
With no water, forever
looks to be arriving a mite too soon.

522
00:52:13,329 --> 00:52:15,058
Why doesn't he do something?

523
00:52:15,464 --> 00:52:18,331
- [Ragetti] Me eye. Give it back!
- There's no sense to it.

524
00:52:18,400 --> 00:52:22,734
And the green flash
happens at sunset, not sunrise.

525
00:52:23,105 --> 00:52:27,769
[Gibbs] "Over the edge." Ah, it's
driving me over the blooming edge.

526
00:52:28,410 --> 00:52:30,378
Sunrises don't set.

527
00:52:34,883 --> 00:52:36,783
[Jack] "Up is down."

528
00:52:36,852 --> 00:52:39,343
Well, that's just maddeningly unhelpful.

529
00:52:39,421 --> 00:52:41,389
Why are these things never clear?

530
00:52:41,457 --> 00:52:44,290
- Clear as mud, Jackie.
- What? Eh?

531
00:52:47,963 --> 00:52:50,454
- Stab the heart.
- Don't stab the heart.

532
00:52:50,532 --> 00:52:53,695
- Come again?
- The Dutchman must have a captain.

533
00:52:53,769 --> 00:52:56,294
That's even more
than less than unhelpful.

534
00:52:56,372 --> 00:52:59,136
Sail the seas for eternity.

535
00:52:59,208 --> 00:53:02,143
- I love the sea.
- What about port?

536
00:53:02,211 --> 00:53:04,202
I prefer rum. Rum's good.

537
00:53:04,280 --> 00:53:09,274
Making port. Where we can get rum,
and salty wenches...

538
00:53:09,685 --> 00:53:13,280
- Once every 10 years.
- What'd he say?

539
00:53:13,355 --> 00:53:16,813
- Once every 10 years.
- Ten years is a long time, mate.

540
00:53:16,892 --> 00:53:19,224
Even longer given the deficit of rum.

541
00:53:19,295 --> 00:53:21,559
But eternity is longer still.

542
00:53:22,298 --> 00:53:25,324
And how will you be spending it? Dead?

543
00:53:26,001 --> 00:53:27,400
Or not?

544
00:53:27,469 --> 00:53:31,337
- The immortal Captain Sparrow.
- Oh, I like that.

545
00:53:31,640 --> 00:53:33,972
Come sunset, it won't matter.

546
00:53:36,779 --> 00:53:38,474
Not sunset.

547
00:53:41,750 --> 00:53:42,944
Sundown.

548
00:53:43,919 --> 00:53:46,444
And rise. Up.

549
00:53:47,723 --> 00:53:48,917
What's that?

550
00:53:51,727 --> 00:53:54,855
What is that?
I don't know. What do you think?

551
00:53:54,930 --> 00:53:56,625
- Where?
- There.

552
00:54:03,405 --> 00:54:05,669
[Jack grunting, stammers]

553
00:54:18,420 --> 00:54:19,751
What is it?

554
00:54:31,800 --> 00:54:33,631
He's rocking the ship.

555
00:54:33,969 --> 00:54:35,596
We're rocking the ship.

556
00:54:39,708 --> 00:54:42,575
Aye. He's onto it.

557
00:54:42,644 --> 00:54:44,805
[creaking]

558
00:54:44,880 --> 00:54:46,609
He's rocking the ship.

559
00:54:46,682 --> 00:54:49,082
We'll tie each other
to the mast upside down,

560
00:54:49,151 --> 00:54:51,813
so when the boat flips
we'll be the right way up.

561
00:54:52,921 --> 00:54:54,445
Time it with the swell.

562
00:54:56,091 --> 00:54:59,788
Loose the cannons, you lazy bilge rats.

563
00:55:01,530 --> 00:55:05,159
Unstow the cargo. Let it shift.

564
00:55:05,834 --> 00:55:07,631
[crew shouting in Chinese]

565
00:55:56,852 --> 00:55:58,479
[ship creaking]

566
00:55:59,988 --> 00:56:02,081
[screaming]

567
00:56:10,132 --> 00:56:11,497
Now up...

568
00:56:13,068 --> 00:56:14,399
...is down.

569
00:57:34,383 --> 00:57:36,248
[coughing]

570
00:57:45,561 --> 00:57:46,585
[squawks]

571
00:57:50,399 --> 00:57:52,390
This was your idea.

572
00:57:53,969 --> 00:57:56,460
Blessed sweet westerlies!

573
00:57:57,606 --> 00:57:58,595
We're back!

574
00:58:02,411 --> 00:58:03,708
It's the sunrise.

575
00:58:16,858 --> 00:58:18,849
- [screeches]
- Parley?

576
00:58:18,927 --> 00:58:21,395
We need our pistols!
Get untied, hurry!

577
00:58:36,845 --> 00:58:38,244
All right, then!

578
00:58:38,313 --> 00:58:41,441
The Brethren Court
is a-gathering at Shipwreck Cove.

579
00:58:41,516 --> 00:58:45,213
And, Jack, you and I are a-going.
There'll be no arguing that point.

580
00:58:45,287 --> 00:58:46,686
I is arguing the point.

581
00:58:46,755 --> 00:58:50,384
If there's pirates a-gathering,
I'm pointing my ship the other way.

582
00:58:50,459 --> 00:58:53,895
The pirates are gathering
to fight Beckett, and you're a pirate.

583
00:58:53,962 --> 00:58:56,260
Fight or not,
you're not running, Jack.

584
00:58:56,331 --> 00:58:59,357
If we don't stand together,
they'll hunt us down one by one,

585
00:58:59,434 --> 00:59:01,061
till there be none left but you.

586
00:59:01,970 --> 00:59:07,431
Quite like the sound of that.
Captain Jack Sparrow, the last pirate.

587
00:59:07,509 --> 00:59:08,533
Aye.

588
00:59:09,044 --> 00:59:13,413
And you'll be fighting Jones alone.
How does that figure into your plan?

589
00:59:13,482 --> 00:59:15,473
I'm still working on that.

590
00:59:15,550 --> 00:59:20,044
But I will not be going back
to the Locker, mate. Count on that.

591
00:59:20,122 --> 00:59:21,146
[clicks]

592
00:59:22,257 --> 00:59:24,521
[guns clicking]

593
00:59:25,727 --> 00:59:27,058
Wet powder.

594
00:59:29,798 --> 00:59:33,325
Wait! We can still use them as clubs!

595
00:59:35,337 --> 00:59:37,862
- Ow!
- Sorry. Effective, though.

596
00:59:38,974 --> 00:59:40,999
There's fresh water on this island.

597
00:59:41,076 --> 00:59:44,705
We can resupply there and get back
to shooting each other later.

598
00:59:45,013 --> 00:59:47,811
You lead the shore party.
I'll stay with my ship.

599
00:59:48,283 --> 00:59:50,717
I'll not be leaving
my ship in your command.

600
00:59:50,786 --> 00:59:54,415
Why don't you both go ashore
and leave the ship in my command.

601
00:59:55,657 --> 00:59:56,988
Temporarily.

602
00:59:59,194 --> 01:00:01,594
[telescope opening]

603
01:00:09,905 --> 01:00:12,373
[shouting]

604
01:00:14,810 --> 01:00:16,368
[squawks] Mind the boat.

605
01:00:27,422 --> 01:00:30,323
- Criminy.
- Odds bodkins!

606
01:00:45,474 --> 01:00:47,806
[seagulls cawing]

607
01:00:49,344 --> 01:00:51,335
Careful! Careful!

608
01:00:52,881 --> 01:00:54,781
You stupid fish!

609
01:00:54,916 --> 01:00:56,884
Actually it's a cephalopod.

610
01:00:57,352 --> 01:00:58,546
Serves you right.

611
01:00:58,687 --> 01:01:01,679
[Ragetti] Pin, I bet people
would pay a shilling to see this.

612
01:01:01,757 --> 01:01:04,920
And another shilling
for a sketch of them sitting atop!

613
01:01:04,993 --> 01:01:08,019
Pintel and Ragetti, Kraken slayers.

614
01:01:08,096 --> 01:01:10,929
[Ragetti] We could serve up
a slice as a souvenir.

615
01:01:10,999 --> 01:01:13,593
Still thinking of running, Jack?

616
01:01:14,369 --> 01:01:16,860
Think you can outrun the world?

617
01:01:22,544 --> 01:01:26,378
You know, the problem
with being the last of anything...

618
01:01:26,448 --> 01:01:29,144
...by and by, there be none left at all.

619
01:01:30,552 --> 01:01:35,546
Sometimes things come back, mate.
We're living proof, you and me.

620
01:01:35,624 --> 01:01:38,752
Aye, but that's a gamble
of long odds, ain't it?

621
01:01:39,461 --> 01:01:42,123
There's never
a guarantee of coming back.

622
01:01:42,631 --> 01:01:45,293
But passing on, that's dead certain.

623
01:01:48,670 --> 01:01:51,662
Summoning the Brethren Court,
then, is it?

624
01:01:51,740 --> 01:01:53,469
It's our only hope, lad.

625
01:01:54,876 --> 01:01:57,868
That's a sad commentary
in and of itself.

626
01:01:59,514 --> 01:02:02,176
The world used to be a bigger place.

627
01:02:04,853 --> 01:02:06,753
The world's still the same.

628
01:02:08,690 --> 01:02:11,454
There's just... less in it.

629
01:02:33,748 --> 01:02:36,080
Poisoned. Fouled by the body.

630
01:02:38,887 --> 01:02:42,687
Eh, I know him. He was in Singapore!

631
01:02:42,757 --> 01:02:44,748
- [squawks] Singapore.
- Captain!

632
01:02:47,596 --> 01:02:48,688
Hoy!

633
01:02:49,764 --> 01:02:51,561
We got company!

634
01:03:03,044 --> 01:03:04,739
He's the captain.

635
01:03:06,548 --> 01:03:08,539
[shouting]

636
01:03:15,190 --> 01:03:16,487
Sao Feng...

637
01:03:16,558 --> 01:03:21,052
...you showing up here,
it is truly a remarkable coincidence.

638
01:03:23,865 --> 01:03:25,492
[Sao Feng] Jack Sparrow.

639
01:03:27,469 --> 01:03:29,562
You paid me great insult once.

640
01:03:29,638 --> 01:03:31,128
That doesn't sound like me.

641
01:03:33,108 --> 01:03:34,507
[groans]

642
01:03:36,044 --> 01:03:38,205
Shall we just call it square, then?

643
01:03:38,747 --> 01:03:39,907
Release her.

644
01:03:40,715 --> 01:03:44,549
- She's not part of the bargain.
- And what bargain be that?

645
01:03:45,420 --> 01:03:47,411
You heard Captain Turner.

646
01:03:48,957 --> 01:03:51,551
- Release her!
- "Captain Turner"?

647
01:03:51,626 --> 01:03:54,390
Aye, the perfidious rotter
led a mutiny against us!

648
01:03:54,462 --> 01:03:57,022
I need the Pearl to free my father.

649
01:03:57,098 --> 01:03:59,589
That's the only reason
I came on this voyage.

650
01:04:00,135 --> 01:04:02,968
Why didn't you tell me
you were planning this?

651
01:04:03,038 --> 01:04:05,233
It was my burden to bear.

652
01:04:07,475 --> 01:04:11,775
He needs the Pearl.
Captain Turner needs the Pearl.

653
01:04:11,846 --> 01:04:15,907
And you felt guilty.
And you and your Brethren Court.

654
01:04:16,952 --> 01:04:19,887
Did no one come to save me
just because they missed me?

655
01:04:29,097 --> 01:04:30,564
I'm standing with them.

656
01:04:32,734 --> 01:04:38,400
I'm sorry, Jack, but there is an
old friend who wants to see you first.

657
01:04:38,473 --> 01:04:42,102
I'm not certain I can survive
any more visits from old friends.

658
01:04:44,512 --> 01:04:46,742
Here is your chance to find out.

659
01:04:56,157 --> 01:04:57,818
Curious.

660
01:04:57,892 --> 01:05:01,055
Your friends appear
to be quite desperate, Jack.

661
01:05:01,129 --> 01:05:05,657
Perhaps they no longer believe that
a gathering of squabbling pirates

662
01:05:05,734 --> 01:05:07,702
can defeat the Flying Dutchman.

663
01:05:08,436 --> 01:05:11,872
And so despair leads to betrayal.

664
01:05:12,273 --> 01:05:16,266
But you and I are no strangers
to betrayal, are we?

665
01:05:19,047 --> 01:05:20,537
It's not here, Jack.

666
01:05:21,950 --> 01:05:23,349
What? What isn't?

667
01:05:23,418 --> 01:05:27,912
The heart of Davy Jones.
It's safely aboard the Dutchman,

668
01:05:27,989 --> 01:05:31,015
and so unavailable for use as leverage

669
01:05:31,092 --> 01:05:33,754
to satisfy your debt
to the good captain.

670
01:05:33,828 --> 01:05:37,093
By my reckoning,
that account has been settled.

671
01:05:37,232 --> 01:05:39,291
[Beckett] By your death?

672
01:05:39,367 --> 01:05:41,426
And yet here you are.

673
01:05:41,970 --> 01:05:44,598
Close your eyes
and pretend it's all a bad dream.

674
01:05:44,672 --> 01:05:46,105
That's how I get by.

675
01:05:47,008 --> 01:05:50,637
And if Davy Jones
were to learn of your survival?

676
01:05:51,246 --> 01:05:52,713
[man] Ship oars.

677
01:05:52,781 --> 01:05:54,339
[man 2] Ship oars.

678
01:05:54,649 --> 01:05:56,241
Be on your guard, men.

679
01:05:58,887 --> 01:06:00,752
My men are crew enough.

680
01:06:00,822 --> 01:06:02,983
Company ship, company crew.

681
01:06:04,059 --> 01:06:06,960
You agreed.
The Black Pearl was to be mine.

682
01:06:07,862 --> 01:06:09,261
And so it was.

683
01:06:12,333 --> 01:06:15,393
[Beckett] Perhaps you'll consider
an alternative arrangement.

684
01:06:15,470 --> 01:06:20,703
One which requires absolutely
nothing from you but information.

685
01:06:27,148 --> 01:06:31,847
Regarding the Brethren Court, no doubt.
In exchange for fair compensation?

686
01:06:32,921 --> 01:06:34,980
Square my debt with Jones...

687
01:06:36,024 --> 01:06:37,889
...guarantee my freedom?

688
01:06:37,959 --> 01:06:39,586
Of course.

689
01:06:39,661 --> 01:06:41,595
It's just good business.

690
01:06:42,664 --> 01:06:47,465
Were I in a divulgatory mood,
what then might I divulge?

691
01:06:47,535 --> 01:06:49,730
Everything.

692
01:06:50,205 --> 01:06:51,502
Where are they meeting?

693
01:06:52,307 --> 01:06:54,298
Who are the pirate lords?

694
01:06:55,276 --> 01:06:58,473
What is the purpose
of the nine pieces of eight?

695
01:06:59,114 --> 01:07:02,242
[man] Prepare the brig.

696
01:07:04,919 --> 01:07:09,822
Beckett agreed the Black Pearl
was to be mine.

697
01:07:09,891 --> 01:07:13,554
He's not going to give up the only ship
as can outrun the Dutchman, is he?

698
01:07:20,702 --> 01:07:25,071
Shame they're not bound to honor
the code of the Brethren, isn't it?

699
01:07:25,140 --> 01:07:28,371
Because honor's
a hard thing to come by nowadays.

700
01:07:29,043 --> 01:07:32,240
There is no honor
to remaining with the losing side.

701
01:07:32,313 --> 01:07:36,841
Leaving it for the winning side,
that's just good business.

702
01:07:36,918 --> 01:07:40,786
- The losing side, you say?
- They have the Dutchman.

703
01:07:40,855 --> 01:07:42,345
Now the Pearl]

704
01:07:43,358 --> 01:07:45,292
And what do the Brethren have?

705
01:07:45,994 --> 01:07:49,054
We have Calypso.

706
01:07:56,838 --> 01:07:59,204
[laughs] Calypso.

707
01:08:01,276 --> 01:08:03,267
An old legend.

708
01:08:04,412 --> 01:08:09,349
No. The goddess herself,
bound in human form.

709
01:08:09,784 --> 01:08:15,051
Imagine all the power of the seas
brought to bear against our enemy.

710
01:08:15,123 --> 01:08:20,425
I intend to release her.
But for that I need the Brethren Court.

711
01:08:22,797 --> 01:08:24,389
All the Court.

712
01:08:26,267 --> 01:08:27,962
You can keep Barbossa.

713
01:08:28,036 --> 01:08:32,097
The belligerent homunculus and
his friend with the wooden eye, both.

714
01:08:32,173 --> 01:08:34,698
And Turner. Especially Turner.

715
01:08:34,776 --> 01:08:38,234
The rest go with me on the Pearl.
I'll lead you to Shipwreck Cove,

716
01:08:38,313 --> 01:08:42,647
where I will hand you the pirates
and you will not hand me to Jones.

717
01:08:42,717 --> 01:08:47,245
- Bloody fair deal, don't you think?
- And what becomes of Miss Swann?

718
01:08:49,991 --> 01:08:52,118
What interest is she to you?

719
01:08:54,662 --> 01:08:57,028
What are you proposing, captain?

720
01:08:57,098 --> 01:09:00,090
What be accepting, cap'n?

721
01:09:01,569 --> 01:09:03,935
- The girl.
- What?

722
01:09:04,005 --> 01:09:05,563
Jack.

723
01:09:06,241 --> 01:09:08,038
I've just recalled.

724
01:09:08,109 --> 01:09:13,103
I've got this wonderful compass,
which points to whatever I want.

725
01:09:13,648 --> 01:09:15,912
So for what do I need you?

726
01:09:15,984 --> 01:09:18,009
Elizabeth is not part of any bargain.

727
01:09:18,820 --> 01:09:20,151
Out of the question.

728
01:09:20,221 --> 01:09:22,018
It was not a question.

729
01:09:22,090 --> 01:09:23,853
Points to the thing you want most.

730
01:09:23,925 --> 01:09:26,120
And that is not
the Brethren Court, is it?

731
01:09:26,194 --> 01:09:29,425
- Then what is, Jack?
- Me.

732
01:09:30,365 --> 01:09:32,526
- Dead.
- Done.

733
01:09:32,600 --> 01:09:35,262
What? Not done.

734
01:09:35,336 --> 01:09:37,896
You got us into this.
If it frees us, then done!

735
01:09:37,972 --> 01:09:39,064
Damn.

736
01:09:43,278 --> 01:09:47,044
Although, if I kill you,
then I can use the compass

737
01:09:47,115 --> 01:09:51,609
to find Shipwreck Cove,
is it, on my own.

738
01:09:51,953 --> 01:09:54,319
Cut out the middle man, as it were.

739
01:09:54,389 --> 01:09:56,118
Elizabeth, they are pirates.

740
01:09:56,190 --> 01:09:59,682
I have had more than enough
experience dealing with pirates!

741
01:10:00,295 --> 01:10:02,661
Then we have an accord?

742
01:10:04,532 --> 01:10:07,092
With me killed,
you'd arrive at the cove,

743
01:10:07,168 --> 01:10:10,365
find it a stronghold,
nigh impregnable...

744
01:10:10,438 --> 01:10:13,373
...able to withstand blockade for years.

745
01:10:13,441 --> 01:10:18,003
Then you'd be wishing, "If only there
was someone I had not killed inside

746
01:10:18,146 --> 01:10:21,582
to ensure that the pirates
then come outside."

747
01:10:22,450 --> 01:10:24,350
And you can accomplish all this?

748
01:10:25,653 --> 01:10:28,713
You may kill me,
but you may never insult me.

749
01:10:29,057 --> 01:10:30,115
Who am I?

750
01:10:32,427 --> 01:10:34,122
I'm Captain Jack Sparrow.

751
01:10:35,163 --> 01:10:36,892
[rumbling]

752
01:10:37,965 --> 01:10:39,125
Done!

753
01:10:49,410 --> 01:10:52,937
- [man shouting]
- [cannon fire]

754
01:11:12,400 --> 01:11:13,560
[man] Man overboard!

755
01:11:27,648 --> 01:11:28,842
You're mad.

756
01:11:29,150 --> 01:11:32,381
Thank goodness.
If I wasn't, this'd probably never work.

757
01:11:36,991 --> 01:11:39,357
[Jack shouting]

758
01:11:50,071 --> 01:11:53,234
And that was without
even a single drop of rum.

759
01:12:00,748 --> 01:12:05,515
Send this pestilent, traitorous,
cowhearted, yeasty codpiece to the brig.

760
01:12:07,755 --> 01:12:09,416
Which ship do we follow?

761
01:12:09,490 --> 01:12:14,154
Signal the Dutchman to track down
Sao Feng. We follow the Pearl.

762
01:12:15,129 --> 01:12:17,654
How soon can we have
the ship ready to pursue?

763
01:12:17,732 --> 01:12:20,064
[wood cracking]

764
01:12:20,535 --> 01:12:21,832
[man] She's going down!

765
01:12:21,903 --> 01:12:23,427
[man 2] Stand clear!

766
01:12:27,542 --> 01:12:32,445
Do you think he plans it all out,
or just makes it up as he goes along?

767
01:12:34,315 --> 01:12:35,304
Sir.

768
01:12:54,435 --> 01:12:57,563
[speaking Chinese]

769
01:13:17,725 --> 01:13:23,721
By this time tomorrow, we will arrive
at Shipwreck Cove and you will be free.

770
01:13:26,267 --> 01:13:27,666
Calypso.

771
01:13:28,769 --> 01:13:30,236
Excuse me?

772
01:13:31,272 --> 01:13:35,368
Not a name you fancy, I imagine,
out of the many that you have...

773
01:13:35,943 --> 01:13:37,570
...but it is what we call you.

774
01:13:39,480 --> 01:13:40,674
We being who?

775
01:13:45,419 --> 01:13:47,216
You confirm it.

776
01:13:48,589 --> 01:13:49,988
Confirm what?

777
01:13:51,259 --> 01:13:55,855
- You've told me nothing.
- The Brethren Court, not I.

778
01:13:55,930 --> 01:14:00,526
The First Brethren Court,
whose decision I would have opposed.

779
01:14:01,636 --> 01:14:03,536
They bound you to human form

780
01:14:03,604 --> 01:14:07,062
so the rule of the seas
would belong to man and not...

781
01:14:07,141 --> 01:14:08,904
To me.

782
01:14:11,479 --> 01:14:16,348
But one such as you should never
be anything less than what you are.

783
01:14:16,417 --> 01:14:19,011
Pretty speech from a captor.

784
01:14:19,086 --> 01:14:23,079
But words whispered
through prison bars lose their charm.

785
01:14:23,424 --> 01:14:26,416
Can I be blamed for my efforts?

786
01:14:28,396 --> 01:14:32,662
All men are drawn to the sea,
perilous though it may be.

787
01:14:32,733 --> 01:14:36,533
And some men offer desire
as justification for their crimes.

788
01:14:37,572 --> 01:14:40,097
I offer simply my desire.

789
01:14:41,576 --> 01:14:43,066
And in return?

790
01:14:44,178 --> 01:14:48,114
I would have your gifts,
should you choose to give them.

791
01:14:52,186 --> 01:14:56,179
And if I should choose not?

792
01:14:57,692 --> 01:14:59,660
Then I will take...

793
01:15:00,995 --> 01:15:02,019
...your fury.

794
01:15:05,132 --> 01:15:06,861
[grunting]

795
01:15:06,934 --> 01:15:08,993
[Elizabeth panting]

796
01:15:16,978 --> 01:15:19,310
[pirates shouting]

797
01:15:27,822 --> 01:15:29,119
Sao Feng?

798
01:15:30,691 --> 01:15:31,817
Here.

799
01:15:32,226 --> 01:15:33,693
Please.

800
01:15:39,567 --> 01:15:43,094
With all nine pieces of eight...

801
01:15:45,573 --> 01:15:47,632
...you will be free.

802
01:15:49,477 --> 01:15:50,603
Take it!

803
01:15:52,446 --> 01:15:54,277
You are captain now.

804
01:15:55,449 --> 01:15:56,473
Me?

805
01:15:59,120 --> 01:16:03,716
Go in my place to Shipwreck Cove.

806
01:16:05,793 --> 01:16:08,387
Captain! The ship is taken.
We cannot...

807
01:16:09,830 --> 01:16:13,391
Forgive me... Calypso.

808
01:16:19,340 --> 01:16:21,467
[explosions and shouting]

809
01:16:22,643 --> 01:16:23,735
Sao.

810
01:16:28,849 --> 01:16:30,544
What did he tell you?

811
01:16:35,623 --> 01:16:37,488
He made me captain.

812
01:16:39,760 --> 01:16:41,990
Set! Fire!

813
01:16:47,435 --> 01:16:49,460
You are not my captain.

814
01:16:49,737 --> 01:16:51,068
Elizabeth.

815
01:16:51,939 --> 01:16:53,497
James. James.

816
01:16:58,245 --> 01:17:00,213
Thank God, you're alive.

817
01:17:00,915 --> 01:17:02,473
Your father'll be overjoyed

818
01:17:02,550 --> 01:17:05,383
- to know you're safe.
- My father's dead.

819
01:17:05,853 --> 01:17:08,583
No, that can't be true.
He returned to England.

820
01:17:09,590 --> 01:17:11,524
Did Lord Beckett tell you that?

821
01:17:12,827 --> 01:17:15,591
Who among you
do you name as captain?

822
01:17:15,663 --> 01:17:17,153
Captain. Her.

823
01:17:17,231 --> 01:17:18,892
[all] Her.

824
01:17:21,769 --> 01:17:22,861
Captain?

825
01:17:22,937 --> 01:17:26,600
Tow the ship.
Put the prisoners in the brig.

826
01:17:27,908 --> 01:17:29,876
The captain shall have my quarters.

827
01:17:31,445 --> 01:17:32,912
Thank you, sir.

828
01:17:32,980 --> 01:17:36,711
- But I prefer to remain with my crew.
- Elizabeth.

829
01:17:36,784 --> 01:17:38,274
I swear.

830
01:17:39,220 --> 01:17:40,278
I did not know.

831
01:17:40,354 --> 01:17:44,484
Know what?
Which side you chose?

832
01:17:52,500 --> 01:17:54,263
Well, now you do.

833
01:18:03,143 --> 01:18:04,132
Bootstrap?

834
01:18:07,381 --> 01:18:09,941
[chuckling]

835
01:18:12,753 --> 01:18:14,050
Bootstrap.

836
01:18:14,555 --> 01:18:15,783
Bill Turner?

837
01:18:16,924 --> 01:18:18,050
[man] Bootstrap.

838
01:18:27,234 --> 01:18:29,134
You know my name.

839
01:18:32,639 --> 01:18:34,004
Yes, I know your son.

840
01:18:36,076 --> 01:18:37,600
Will Turner.

841
01:18:39,046 --> 01:18:40,138
William.

842
01:18:46,020 --> 01:18:48,511
He made it. He's alive.

843
01:18:48,589 --> 01:18:52,821
And now he sends you to tell me
that he's coming to get me.

844
01:18:54,795 --> 01:18:57,161
God's wounds. He's on his way.

845
01:18:57,231 --> 01:18:58,596
Yes, Will is alive.

846
01:19:00,034 --> 01:19:01,592
And he wants to help you.

847
01:19:07,074 --> 01:19:10,703
He can't help me. He won't come.

848
01:19:11,478 --> 01:19:13,036
But you're his father.

849
01:19:13,881 --> 01:19:15,644
I know you.

850
01:19:16,483 --> 01:19:18,041
He spoke of you.

851
01:19:20,020 --> 01:19:23,183
He can't save me.
He can't come because of you.

852
01:19:24,558 --> 01:19:25,582
Me?

853
01:19:27,127 --> 01:19:28,856
You're Elizabeth.

854
01:19:30,230 --> 01:19:31,697
Yes, I'm Elizabeth.

855
01:19:33,500 --> 01:19:38,460
If Jones be slain,
he who slays him must take his place.

856
01:19:39,039 --> 01:19:41,906
Captain... Forever.

857
01:19:44,778 --> 01:19:47,747
The Dutchman
must always have a captain.

858
01:19:48,449 --> 01:19:50,542
And if he saves me...

859
01:19:51,752 --> 01:19:53,617
...he loses you.

860
01:19:56,590 --> 01:19:57,682
I see.

861
01:19:59,660 --> 01:20:01,525
He won't pick me.

862
01:20:02,463 --> 01:20:04,624
I wouldn't pick me.

863
01:20:09,703 --> 01:20:11,227
Tell him not to come.

864
01:20:13,440 --> 01:20:15,567
Tell him to stay away.

865
01:20:16,877 --> 01:20:18,902
Tell him it's too late.

866
01:20:20,481 --> 01:20:22,745
I'm already a part of the ship...

867
01:20:24,818 --> 01:20:26,445
...and the crew.

868
01:20:28,622 --> 01:20:29,611
Bootstrap.

869
01:20:32,693 --> 01:20:34,593
You know my name.

870
01:20:35,496 --> 01:20:37,589
You know my name.

871
01:20:38,832 --> 01:20:40,663
Yes, I know your son.

872
01:20:41,668 --> 01:20:43,363
William.

873
01:20:45,973 --> 01:20:47,941
He's coming for me.

874
01:20:48,642 --> 01:20:50,132
Wait and see.

875
01:20:51,245 --> 01:20:52,576
You'll see.

876
01:20:53,981 --> 01:20:55,744
He promised.

877
01:21:12,166 --> 01:21:14,100
[man] Swing her over.

878
01:21:15,302 --> 01:21:17,065
Ready those lines.

879
01:21:28,815 --> 01:21:29,975
Sir.

880
01:21:33,353 --> 01:21:35,218
[Beckett] A breadcrumb trail.

881
01:21:35,289 --> 01:21:39,623
- And we're meant to follow.
- A betrayer among them?

882
01:21:39,693 --> 01:21:44,858
- Or a trap?
- A gambit by a skilled opponent.

883
01:21:46,166 --> 01:21:50,159
- Adjust course, lieutenant.
- Sir?

884
01:21:52,005 --> 01:21:54,269
We can only hope
to reach our destination

885
01:21:54,341 --> 01:21:56,366
before they run out of bodies.

886
01:22:10,023 --> 01:22:13,083
You escaped the brig
even quicker than I expected.

887
01:22:14,027 --> 01:22:17,428
William, do you notice anything?

888
01:22:19,099 --> 01:22:20,327
Rather...

889
01:22:20,734 --> 01:22:24,261
...do you notice something
that is not there to be noticed?

890
01:22:25,038 --> 01:22:28,030
- You haven't raised an alarm.
- Odd, isn't it?

891
01:22:28,108 --> 01:22:29,575
Not as odd as this.

892
01:22:30,978 --> 01:22:33,446
Come up with this
all by your Ionesome, did you?

893
01:22:33,513 --> 01:22:36,539
I said to myself, "Think like Jack."

894
01:22:36,917 --> 01:22:39,112
This is what you've arrived at?

895
01:22:39,186 --> 01:22:42,519
Lead Beckett to Shipwreck Cove
so as to gain his trust...

896
01:22:42,589 --> 01:22:44,420
...accomplish your own ends?

897
01:22:44,491 --> 01:22:46,288
It's like you don't know me at all.

898
01:22:49,596 --> 01:22:52,690
And how does your dearly beloved
feel about this plan?

899
01:22:54,067 --> 01:22:55,398
Ah.

900
01:22:56,003 --> 01:22:58,528
You've not seen fit
to trust her with it.

901
01:23:01,908 --> 01:23:03,466
I'm losing her, Jack.

902
01:23:04,211 --> 01:23:08,147
Every step I make for my father
is a step away from Elizabeth.

903
01:23:08,615 --> 01:23:12,312
If you choose to lock your heart away,
you'll lose her for certain.

904
01:23:12,853 --> 01:23:16,050
If I might lend a machete
to your intellectual thicket...

905
01:23:17,224 --> 01:23:19,192
...avoid the choice altogether.

906
01:23:20,027 --> 01:23:21,494
Change the facts.

907
01:23:22,562 --> 01:23:25,554
Let someone else dispatch Jones.

908
01:23:26,333 --> 01:23:27,664
Who?

909
01:23:31,938 --> 01:23:32,927
You?

910
01:23:35,275 --> 01:23:38,676
Death has a curious way
of reshuffling one's priorities.

911
01:23:38,745 --> 01:23:42,272
I slip aboard the Dutchman,
find the heart, stab the beating thing,

912
01:23:42,349 --> 01:23:44,112
your father's free from his debt,

913
01:23:44,184 --> 01:23:46,914
you're free to be
with your charming murderess.

914
01:23:46,987 --> 01:23:50,821
You're willing to cut out your heart
and bind yourself to the Dutchman...

915
01:23:50,891 --> 01:23:54,850
...forever?
- No, mate. I'm free forever.

916
01:23:54,928 --> 01:23:58,955
Free to sail the seas beyond the edges
of the map. Free from death itself.

917
01:23:59,299 --> 01:24:01,358
You have to do the job though, Jack.

918
01:24:01,435 --> 01:24:04,495
You have to ferry souls
to the next world.

919
01:24:05,138 --> 01:24:07,732
- Or end up just like Jones.
- [groans]

920
01:24:09,810 --> 01:24:11,801
I don't have the face for tentacles.

921
01:24:13,647 --> 01:24:16,207
But immortal has to
count for something, eh?

922
01:24:20,220 --> 01:24:21,380
What's this for?

923
01:24:21,455 --> 01:24:24,151
Think like me. It'll come to you.

924
01:24:37,404 --> 01:24:39,804
My regards to Davy Jones.

925
01:24:45,078 --> 01:24:46,545
I hate him.

926
01:24:51,118 --> 01:24:52,244
Come with me.

927
01:24:55,822 --> 01:24:57,050
Quickly.

928
01:25:06,099 --> 01:25:07,361
What are you doing?

929
01:25:09,035 --> 01:25:11,060
Choosing a side.

930
01:25:30,290 --> 01:25:32,281
[whispering in Chinese]

931
01:25:35,595 --> 01:25:39,554
Do not go to Shipwreck Cove. Beckett
knows of the meeting of the Brethren.

932
01:25:39,633 --> 01:25:41,965
I fear there may be
a traitor among them.

933
01:25:43,303 --> 01:25:45,430
It's too late to earn my forgiveness.

934
01:25:46,673 --> 01:25:49,471
I had nothing to do
with your father's death.

935
01:25:51,678 --> 01:25:54,670
But that does not absolve me
of my other sins.

936
01:25:58,218 --> 01:25:59,276
Come with us.

937
01:26:03,356 --> 01:26:05,051
James, come with me.

938
01:26:06,259 --> 01:26:08,159
[Bootstrap Bill] Who goes there?

939
01:26:10,931 --> 01:26:12,865
Go. I will follow.

940
01:26:14,468 --> 01:26:16,129
You're lying.

941
01:26:18,872 --> 01:26:21,773
Our destinies have been entwined,
Elizabeth...

942
01:26:24,144 --> 01:26:25,668
...but never joined.

943
01:26:32,185 --> 01:26:33,880
Go, now.

944
01:26:39,860 --> 01:26:42,021
Back to your station, sailor.

945
01:26:43,864 --> 01:26:49,131
- No one leaves the ship.
- Stand down. That's an order.

946
01:26:49,202 --> 01:26:50,897
That's an order.

947
01:26:51,972 --> 01:26:54,634
Part of the crew, part of the ship.

948
01:26:54,708 --> 01:26:57,472
Part of the crew,
part of the ship. Part of the crew...

949
01:26:57,544 --> 01:27:00,445
- Steady, man.
- Part of the crew, part of the ship.

950
01:27:00,514 --> 01:27:02,914
All hands, prisoner escape!

951
01:27:03,250 --> 01:27:05,480
- Belay that!
- James!

952
01:27:12,792 --> 01:27:14,919
[screaming]

953
01:27:17,230 --> 01:27:19,323
[man] Prisoner escape!

954
01:27:21,768 --> 01:27:23,429
James! No!

955
01:27:32,512 --> 01:27:35,310
- The admiral's dead.
- Admiral's dead?

956
01:27:35,382 --> 01:27:37,407
- The admiral's dead.
- Admiral's dead.

957
01:27:40,620 --> 01:27:43,180
- To the captain's cabin!
- [all] Aye!

958
01:27:43,256 --> 01:27:45,247
Back to the ship.

959
01:27:47,227 --> 01:27:51,163
James Norrington. Do you fear death?

960
01:28:00,507 --> 01:28:02,566
I take that as a "no."

961
01:28:07,080 --> 01:28:08,604
Nice sword.

962
01:28:29,569 --> 01:28:34,597
The Dutchman is under my command.

963
01:28:37,110 --> 01:28:39,408
For now.

964
01:28:55,194 --> 01:28:57,253
Look alive and keep a weather eye.

965
01:28:57,330 --> 01:28:59,730
Not for naught
it's called Shipwreck Island.

966
01:28:59,799 --> 01:29:03,235
Where lies Shipwreck Cove
and the town of Shipwreck.

967
01:29:03,302 --> 01:29:05,736
You heard him. Step lively.

968
01:29:05,805 --> 01:29:08,137
For all that pirates are clever clogs,

969
01:29:08,207 --> 01:29:11,040
we are unimaginative
when it comes to naming things.

970
01:29:11,110 --> 01:29:12,338
Aye.

971
01:29:12,412 --> 01:29:16,542
I once sailed with a geezer who lost
both of his arms and part of his eye.

972
01:29:16,616 --> 01:29:18,208
What'd you call him?

973
01:29:19,385 --> 01:29:20,613
Larry.

974
01:29:24,323 --> 01:29:27,292
I do not renege
on a bargain once struck.

975
01:29:27,360 --> 01:29:29,351
But we agreed on ends only.

976
01:29:29,429 --> 01:29:32,398
The means are mine to decide.

977
01:29:32,465 --> 01:29:34,956
Caution, Barbossa.

978
01:29:35,034 --> 01:29:39,266
Do not forget it was by my power
you return from the dead.

979
01:29:39,338 --> 01:29:43,172
Or what it mean if you fail me.

980
01:29:46,446 --> 01:29:49,313
Don't you forget
why you had to bring me back.

981
01:29:49,382 --> 01:29:53,113
Why I could not leave Jack
to his well-deserved fate.

982
01:29:54,020 --> 01:29:56,545
It took nine pirate lords
to bind you, Calypso.

983
01:29:57,090 --> 01:30:00,389
And it'll take no less than nine
to set you free.

984
01:30:00,460 --> 01:30:02,655
Masters Pintel and Ragetti.

985
01:30:04,497 --> 01:30:07,591
Take this fishwife to the brig.

986
01:30:09,435 --> 01:30:11,562
Right this way, Mrs. Fish.

987
01:30:39,398 --> 01:30:43,391
I cannot be summoned
like some mongrel pup.

988
01:30:43,469 --> 01:30:45,403
Apparently, you can.

989
01:30:45,938 --> 01:30:47,599
I believe you know each other.

990
01:30:50,243 --> 01:30:52,143
[chuckles]

991
01:30:52,211 --> 01:30:56,341
Come to join my crew again,
Master Turner?

992
01:30:56,415 --> 01:30:57,780
Not yours.

993
01:30:58,217 --> 01:30:59,411
His.

994
01:31:00,319 --> 01:31:02,446
Jack Sparrow sends his regards.

995
01:31:03,556 --> 01:31:05,387
Sparrow?

996
01:31:06,826 --> 01:31:08,418
You didn't tell him.

997
01:31:08,494 --> 01:31:11,725
We rescued Jack from the Locker
along with the Black Pearl.

998
01:31:12,498 --> 01:31:15,399
What else have you not told me?

999
01:31:16,169 --> 01:31:20,162
There is an issue far more troublesome.

1000
01:31:21,507 --> 01:31:25,102
I believe you're familiar
with a person called Calypso.

1001
01:31:27,747 --> 01:31:29,442
Not a person.

1002
01:31:30,183 --> 01:31:31,241
A heathen god.

1003
01:31:31,317 --> 01:31:34,844
One who delights in cursing men
with their wildest dreams

1004
01:31:34,921 --> 01:31:38,254
and then revealing them to be
hollow and naught but ash.

1005
01:31:38,324 --> 01:31:41,452
- The world is well rid of her.
- Not quite so well, actually.

1006
01:31:42,361 --> 01:31:44,955
The Brethren Court
intends to release her.

1007
01:31:45,765 --> 01:31:47,562
No! They cannot!

1008
01:31:47,633 --> 01:31:50,602
The First Court
promised to imprison her forever.

1009
01:31:50,670 --> 01:31:53,639
- That was our agreement.
- Your agreement?

1010
01:31:56,175 --> 01:32:00,168
I showed them how to bind her.

1011
01:32:00,246 --> 01:32:02,214
She could not be trusted.

1012
01:32:02,281 --> 01:32:04,112
I... She gave me no choice.

1013
01:32:04,684 --> 01:32:07,619
We must act before they release her.

1014
01:32:07,687 --> 01:32:09,450
You loved her.

1015
01:32:10,690 --> 01:32:12,658
She's the one.

1016
01:32:12,725 --> 01:32:14,716
And then you betrayed her.

1017
01:32:14,794 --> 01:32:18,594
She pretended to love me.

1018
01:32:18,664 --> 01:32:22,430
She betrayed me.

1019
01:32:25,271 --> 01:32:28,798
And after which betrayal
did you cut out your heart, I wonder.

1020
01:32:32,078 --> 01:32:34,774
Do not test me.

1021
01:32:34,847 --> 01:32:36,576
I hadn't finished that.

1022
01:32:38,584 --> 01:32:40,711
You will free my father.

1023
01:32:41,420 --> 01:32:43,888
And you will guarantee
Elizabeth's safety.

1024
01:32:43,956 --> 01:32:45,548
Along with my own.

1025
01:32:45,625 --> 01:32:48,185
Your terms are steep, Mr. Turner.

1026
01:32:48,261 --> 01:32:51,025
We will expect fair value in return.

1027
01:32:51,464 --> 01:32:55,400
There is only one price I will accept:

1028
01:32:55,468 --> 01:32:58,528
Calypso murdered.

1029
01:33:00,439 --> 01:33:02,771
Calypso's aboard the Black Pearl.

1030
01:33:04,844 --> 01:33:08,211
Jack has sailed
the Black Pearl to Shipwreck Cove.

1031
01:33:09,248 --> 01:33:14,481
And with you no longer aboard her,
how do you propose to lead us there?

1032
01:33:26,332 --> 01:33:28,892
What is it you want most?

1033
01:34:00,833 --> 01:34:02,391
Look at them all.

1034
01:34:02,468 --> 01:34:06,564
There's not been a gathering
like this in our lifetime.

1035
01:34:07,373 --> 01:34:09,364
And I owe them all money.

1036
01:34:11,043 --> 01:34:13,511
[murmuring]

1037
01:34:14,780 --> 01:34:17,248
As he who issued summons,

1038
01:34:17,316 --> 01:34:21,878
I convene this,
the Fourth Brethren Court.

1039
01:34:21,954 --> 01:34:24,752
[murmuring]

1040
01:34:25,958 --> 01:34:29,416
To confirm your lordship
and right to be heard,

1041
01:34:29,528 --> 01:34:34,261
present now your pieces of eight,
my fellow cap'ns.

1042
01:34:42,408 --> 01:34:45,605
Those aren't pieces of eight.
They're just pieces of junk.

1043
01:34:45,678 --> 01:34:49,409
Aye. The original plan was to use
nine pieces of eight to bind Calypso.

1044
01:34:49,482 --> 01:34:53,816
But when the First Court met,
the Brethren were to a one skint broke.

1045
01:34:54,520 --> 01:34:55,578
So change the name.

1046
01:34:56,789 --> 01:35:00,987
To "nine pieces of whatever we happened
to have in our pockets at the time?"

1047
01:35:01,060 --> 01:35:03,392
Oh, yes. That sounds very piratey.

1048
01:35:07,033 --> 01:35:09,524
Master Ragetti, if you will.

1049
01:35:10,336 --> 01:35:13,328
I kept it safe, just like
you said when you gave it to me.

1050
01:35:13,406 --> 01:35:16,500
Aye, you have.
But now I need it back.

1051
01:35:19,378 --> 01:35:20,470
Sparrow.

1052
01:35:26,585 --> 01:35:30,214
Might I point out that
we are still short one pirate lord,

1053
01:35:30,289 --> 01:35:34,248
and I'm as content as a cucumber
to wait until Sao Feng joins us.

1054
01:35:34,326 --> 01:35:35,918
[Elizabeth] Sao Feng is dead.

1055
01:35:38,064 --> 01:35:40,157
He fell to the Flying Dutchman.

1056
01:35:40,399 --> 01:35:41,525
The plagued ship.

1057
01:35:42,768 --> 01:35:46,169
He made you captain? They're
giving the bloody title away now.

1058
01:35:46,238 --> 01:35:49,207
- {Qu?lo manden al diablo]
- Listen. Listen to me.

1059
01:35:49,275 --> 01:35:51,505
Our location has been betrayed.

1060
01:35:51,577 --> 01:35:55,206
Jones is under the command of
Lord Beckett. They're on their way here.

1061
01:35:55,281 --> 01:35:58,978
- Who is this betrayer?
- Not likely anyone among us.

1062
01:35:59,351 --> 01:36:01,376
- Where's Will?
- Not among us.

1063
01:36:01,454 --> 01:36:04,184
It matters not how they found us.
The question is,

1064
01:36:04,256 --> 01:36:06,747
what will we do now that they have?

1065
01:36:06,826 --> 01:36:08,316
We fight.

1066
01:36:12,398 --> 01:36:15,834
Shipwreck Cove is a fortress.

1067
01:36:15,901 --> 01:36:18,699
A well-supplied fortress.

1068
01:36:19,305 --> 01:36:23,105
There is no need to fight
if they cannot get to us.

1069
01:36:23,876 --> 01:36:27,243
There be a third course.

1070
01:36:28,481 --> 01:36:30,915
In another age, at this very spot,

1071
01:36:30,983 --> 01:36:36,478
the First Brethren Court captured the
sea goddess and bound her in her bones.

1072
01:36:39,225 --> 01:36:40,658
That was a mistake.

1073
01:36:41,660 --> 01:36:44,424
Oh, we tamed the seas
for ourselves, aye.

1074
01:36:45,397 --> 01:36:48,491
But opened the door
to Beckett and his ilk.

1075
01:36:50,503 --> 01:36:52,937
Better were the days
when mastery of seas

1076
01:36:53,005 --> 01:36:56,839
came not from bargains
struck with eldritch creatures,

1077
01:36:56,909 --> 01:37:01,278
but from the sweat of a man's brow
and the strength of his back alone.

1078
01:37:01,347 --> 01:37:03,178
You all know this to be true.

1079
01:37:04,016 --> 01:37:05,381
Gentlemen.

1080
01:37:06,485 --> 01:37:07,645
Ladies.

1081
01:37:12,291 --> 01:37:15,283
We must free Calypso.

1082
01:37:22,401 --> 01:37:24,232
[clamoring]

1083
01:37:24,303 --> 01:37:26,737
- Shoot him.
- Cut out his tongue!

1084
01:37:26,805 --> 01:37:30,707
Shoot him, cut out his tongue, then
shoot his tongue. And trim that beard.

1085
01:37:30,776 --> 01:37:33,301
Sao Feng would've agreed with Barbossa.

1086
01:37:33,379 --> 01:37:36,280
Calypso was our enemy then,
she will be our enemy now.

1087
01:37:36,348 --> 01:37:38,282
It's unlikely her mood's improved.

1088
01:37:38,350 --> 01:37:42,184
I would still agree with Sao Feng.
We release Calypso.

1089
01:37:42,254 --> 01:37:45,189
- You threaten me?
- I silence you.

1090
01:37:46,158 --> 01:37:48,353
[all shouting]

1091
01:37:57,436 --> 01:38:00,599
- This is madness.
- This is politics.

1092
01:38:00,673 --> 01:38:03,267
Meanwhile, our enemies
are bearing down upon us.

1093
01:38:03,342 --> 01:38:05,867
If they not be here already.

1094
01:38:05,945 --> 01:38:06,011
[shouting and gunfire in distance]

1095
01:38:06,011 --> 01:38:08,411
[shouting and gunfire in distance]

1096
01:38:11,750 --> 01:38:16,744
[music box playing]

1097
01:38:28,000 --> 01:38:29,331
[music box stops]

1098
01:38:29,401 --> 01:38:32,529
[another music box playing]

1099
01:38:42,081 --> 01:38:44,447
My sweet.

1100
01:38:44,750 --> 01:38:46,877
You come for me.

1101
01:38:47,853 --> 01:38:52,813
- You were expecting me.
- It has been torture.

1102
01:38:52,891 --> 01:38:57,123
Trapped in this single form.

1103
01:38:57,663 --> 01:39:00,029
Cut off from the sea.

1104
01:39:00,499 --> 01:39:03,093
From all that I love.

1105
01:39:04,370 --> 01:39:05,769
From you.

1106
01:39:06,472 --> 01:39:11,000
Ten years I devoted
to the duty you charged to me.

1107
01:39:12,378 --> 01:39:17,782
Ten years I looked after
those who died at sea.

1108
01:39:18,450 --> 01:39:23,012
And, finally,
when we could be together again...

1109
01:39:24,790 --> 01:39:28,590
...you weren't there.
- [music box stops]

1110
01:39:28,994 --> 01:39:31,588
Why weren't you there?

1111
01:39:35,000 --> 01:39:36,627
It's my nature.

1112
01:39:37,836 --> 01:39:42,432
Would you love me
if I was anything but what I am?

1113
01:39:43,609 --> 01:39:47,409
I do not love you.

1114
01:39:49,915 --> 01:39:53,612
Many things you were, Davy Jones.

1115
01:39:54,687 --> 01:39:56,814
But never cruel.

1116
01:39:57,656 --> 01:40:00,625
You have corrupted your purpose...

1117
01:40:01,994 --> 01:40:04,588
...and so your self.

1118
01:40:05,664 --> 01:40:10,533
And you did hide away
what should always have been mine.

1119
01:40:10,602 --> 01:40:13,002
[gasping]

1120
01:40:30,422 --> 01:40:33,482
Calypso.

1121
01:40:36,528 --> 01:40:38,393
I will be free.

1122
01:40:40,332 --> 01:40:44,029
And when I am,
I would give you my heart.

1123
01:40:44,803 --> 01:40:47,795
And we would be together always.

1124
01:40:48,440 --> 01:40:51,807
If only you had a heart to give.

1125
01:40:56,582 --> 01:40:58,641
Why did you come?

1126
01:41:15,934 --> 01:41:21,099
And what fate have you planned
for your captors?

1127
01:41:21,507 --> 01:41:23,498
The Brethren Court?

1128
01:41:25,477 --> 01:41:30,278
All of them, the last thing
they will learn in this life...

1129
01:41:30,349 --> 01:41:33,045
...is how cruel I can be.

1130
01:41:37,623 --> 01:41:42,083
And what of your fate, Davy Jones?

1131
01:41:43,562 --> 01:41:47,054
My heart will always belong to you.

1132
01:41:53,272 --> 01:41:55,604
- [shouting]
- [glass breaking]

1133
01:42:00,446 --> 01:42:04,246
It was the First Court
what imprisoned Calypso.

1134
01:42:04,316 --> 01:42:06,614
We should be the ones to set her free.

1135
01:42:06,685 --> 01:42:11,019
And in her gratitude,
she will see fit to grant us boons.

1136
01:42:11,089 --> 01:42:13,557
Whose boons? Your boons?

1137
01:42:13,625 --> 01:42:16,617
Utterly deceptive twaddle-speak, says I.

1138
01:42:16,695 --> 01:42:21,098
If you have a better alternative,
please, share.

1139
01:42:22,367 --> 01:42:23,391
Cuttlefish.

1140
01:42:24,603 --> 01:42:26,662
Aye.

1141
01:42:27,639 --> 01:42:30,267
Let us not, dear friends,

1142
01:42:30,342 --> 01:42:33,334
forget our dear friends, the cuttlefish.

1143
01:42:33,879 --> 01:42:36,074
Flipping glorious little sausages.

1144
01:42:36,148 --> 01:42:40,107
Pen them up together, they'll devour
each other without a second thought.

1145
01:42:40,185 --> 01:42:41,447
Human nature, isn't it?

1146
01:42:41,520 --> 01:42:43,545
Or... Or fish nature.

1147
01:42:43,622 --> 01:42:47,353
So, yes, we could hole up here
well-provisioned and well-armed.

1148
01:42:47,426 --> 01:42:49,587
Half of us would be dead
within the month.

1149
01:42:49,661 --> 01:42:53,597
Which seems quite grim to me,
any way you slice it. Or...

1150
01:42:55,467 --> 01:42:56,798
Uh...

1151
01:42:58,537 --> 01:43:04,305
As my learned colleague
so naively suggests,

1152
01:43:04,376 --> 01:43:08,369
we can release Calypso, and we
can pray that she will be merciful.

1153
01:43:09,882 --> 01:43:11,144
I rather doubt it.

1154
01:43:11,950 --> 01:43:15,943
Can we pretend she's anything
other than a woman scorned

1155
01:43:16,021 --> 01:43:18,888
like which fury hell hath no? We cannot.

1156
01:43:20,058 --> 01:43:23,323
Res ipsa loquitur, tabula in naufragio.

1157
01:43:23,395 --> 01:43:26,159
We are left with but one option.

1158
01:43:26,231 --> 01:43:30,099
I agree with, and I cannot believe
the words are coming out of me mouth...

1159
01:43:32,404 --> 01:43:35,339
...Captain Swann. We must fight.

1160
01:43:35,407 --> 01:43:38,638
- You've always run away from a fight.
- Have not.

1161
01:43:38,710 --> 01:43:39,768
- Have so.
- Have not.

1162
01:43:39,912 --> 01:43:40,970
- Have so.
- Have not!

1163
01:43:41,113 --> 01:43:44,276
- You have so, and you know it.
- Have not. Slander and calumny.

1164
01:43:44,349 --> 01:43:48,649
I have only ever embraced that
oldest and noblest of pirate traditions.

1165
01:43:48,720 --> 01:43:52,918
I submit that here now
that is what we all must do:

1166
01:43:52,991 --> 01:43:54,390
We must fight...

1167
01:43:55,160 --> 01:43:56,650
...to run away.

1168
01:43:56,728 --> 01:43:59,060
- Aye!
- [all] Aye!

1169
01:44:00,832 --> 01:44:05,269
As per the code, an act of war,
and this be exactly that,

1170
01:44:05,337 --> 01:44:08,238
can only be declared by the pirate king.

1171
01:44:08,307 --> 01:44:10,867
- You made that up.
- Did I, now?

1172
01:44:10,943 --> 01:44:15,471
I call on Cap'n Teague,
keeper of the code.

1173
01:44:16,481 --> 01:44:19,917
Sri Sumbhajee proclaims
this all to be folly!

1174
01:44:21,553 --> 01:44:24,044
- Hang the code. Who cares a...?
- [gunshot]

1175
01:44:32,064 --> 01:44:33,895
Code is the law.

1176
01:44:45,911 --> 01:44:47,902
You're in my way, boy.

1177
01:44:57,122 --> 01:44:59,750
- The code.
- The code.

1178
01:44:59,825 --> 01:45:02,089
As set forth by Morgan and Bartholomew.

1179
01:45:13,005 --> 01:45:14,302
[whistles]

1180
01:45:20,112 --> 01:45:24,071
- That can't... How did...
- Sea turtles, mate.

1181
01:45:24,149 --> 01:45:25,480
[barks]

1182
01:45:26,952 --> 01:45:28,442
Sea turtles.

1183
01:45:43,235 --> 01:45:45,931
Ah. Barbossa is right.

1184
01:45:46,004 --> 01:45:47,494
[Jack] Hang on a minute.

1185
01:45:48,974 --> 01:45:50,942
"It shall be the duties, as the king,

1186
01:45:51,009 --> 01:45:54,467
to declare war,
parley with shared adversaries..."

1187
01:45:54,546 --> 01:45:55,535
Fancy that.

1188
01:45:55,614 --> 01:45:58,082
There has not been a king
since the First Court.

1189
01:45:58,150 --> 01:45:59,811
And that's not likely to change.

1190
01:45:59,885 --> 01:46:01,910
- Not likely.
- Why not?

1191
01:46:02,054 --> 01:46:04,682
See, the pirate king
is elected by popular vote.

1192
01:46:04,756 --> 01:46:08,248
And each pirate
only ever votes for hisself.

1193
01:46:08,326 --> 01:46:09,418
I call for a vote.

1194
01:46:10,896 --> 01:46:13,057
- [murmuring]
- [playing guitar]

1195
01:46:14,232 --> 01:46:16,598
I vote for Ammand the corsair.

1196
01:46:16,668 --> 01:46:19,228
Capitaine Chevalle,
the penniless Frenchman.

1197
01:46:19,604 --> 01:46:22,266
Sri Sumbhajee
votes for Sri Sumbhajee.

1198
01:46:23,175 --> 01:46:25,109
Mistress Ching.

1199
01:46:25,177 --> 01:46:26,576
Gentleman Jocard.

1200
01:46:27,279 --> 01:46:29,839
- Elizabeth Swann.
- Barbossa.

1201
01:46:29,915 --> 01:46:31,678
Vallenueva!

1202
01:46:32,517 --> 01:46:33,848
Elizabeth Swann.

1203
01:46:34,886 --> 01:46:36,820
- What?
- I know. Curious, isn't it?

1204
01:46:39,357 --> 01:46:40,984
Vote for me. Vote for me.

1205
01:46:43,462 --> 01:46:44,861
What you talking about?

1206
01:46:44,930 --> 01:46:46,022
No!

1207
01:46:46,098 --> 01:46:49,727
Am I to understand that you lot
will not be keeping to the code, then?

1208
01:46:49,801 --> 01:46:51,996
[string breaks]

1209
01:46:58,610 --> 01:46:59,804
Very well.

1210
01:47:03,181 --> 01:47:08,175
What say you, Captain Swann,
king of the Brethren Court?

1211
01:47:10,155 --> 01:47:12,487
Prepare every vessel that floats.

1212
01:47:13,325 --> 01:47:14,485
At dawn...

1213
01:47:16,061 --> 01:47:17,255
...we're at war.

1214
01:47:21,066 --> 01:47:24,661
[high-pitched]
And so we shall go to war.

1215
01:47:24,736 --> 01:47:27,864
[all shouting]

1216
01:47:38,350 --> 01:47:39,339
What?

1217
01:47:40,318 --> 01:47:43,116
You've seen it all,
done it all. You survived.

1218
01:47:43,655 --> 01:47:46,488
That's the trick, isn't it?
To survive?

1219
01:47:47,058 --> 01:47:50,516
It's not just about
living forever, Jackie.

1220
01:47:54,466 --> 01:47:58,562
The trick is
living with yourself forever.

1221
01:48:02,607 --> 01:48:03,767
How's Mum?

1222
01:48:10,715 --> 01:48:12,239
She looks great.

1223
01:48:33,672 --> 01:48:37,267
The enemy is here! Let's take them!

1224
01:48:37,509 --> 01:48:39,568
[shouting]

1225
01:48:50,655 --> 01:48:51,986
[shouting stops]

1226
01:49:00,632 --> 01:49:03,123
[parrot] Abandon ship. Abandon ship.

1227
01:49:12,077 --> 01:49:13,237
Parley?

1228
01:50:10,035 --> 01:50:13,664
You be the cur that led
these wolves to our door.

1229
01:50:13,738 --> 01:50:17,936
Don't blame Turner.
He was merely the tool of your betrayal.

1230
01:50:18,009 --> 01:50:22,537
If you wish to see its grand architect,
look to your left.

1231
01:50:25,951 --> 01:50:27,646
My hands are clean in this.

1232
01:50:28,687 --> 01:50:29,847
Figuratively.

1233
01:50:30,388 --> 01:50:32,856
My actions were my own
and to my own purpose.

1234
01:50:32,924 --> 01:50:36,360
- Jack had nothing to do with it.
- Well spoke. Listen to the tool.

1235
01:50:36,428 --> 01:50:38,396
Will, I've been aboard the Dutchman.

1236
01:50:39,331 --> 01:50:42,664
I understand the burden you bear,
but I fear that cause is lost.

1237
01:50:43,501 --> 01:50:47,369
No cause is lost if there is
but one fool left to fight for it.

1238
01:50:49,607 --> 01:50:54,510
If Turner wasn't acting on your behalf,
then how did he come to give me this?

1239
01:50:55,714 --> 01:51:00,674
You made a deal with me, Jack, to
deliver the pirates. And here they are.

1240
01:51:01,453 --> 01:51:04,820
Don't be bashful. Step up.
Claim your reward.

1241
01:51:04,889 --> 01:51:08,791
[Jones] Your debt to me
is still to be satisfied.

1242
01:51:08,860 --> 01:51:13,820
One hundred years in servitude
aboard the Dutchman. As a start.

1243
01:51:14,499 --> 01:51:17,730
That debt was paid, mate.
With some help.

1244
01:51:17,802 --> 01:51:20,202
- [Jones] You escaped.
- Technically.

1245
01:51:20,271 --> 01:51:22,330
I propose an exchange.

1246
01:51:24,409 --> 01:51:26,274
Will leaves with us...

1247
01:51:27,012 --> 01:51:28,775
...and you can take Jack.

1248
01:51:30,882 --> 01:51:31,871
Done.

1249
01:51:32,550 --> 01:51:34,245
- Undone.
- Done.

1250
01:51:34,319 --> 01:51:37,846
Jack's one of the nine pirate lords.
You have no right.

1251
01:51:37,922 --> 01:51:39,389
King.

1252
01:51:41,893 --> 01:51:45,954
- As you command, your nibs.
- Blackguard!

1253
01:51:51,169 --> 01:51:54,866
If ye have something to say,
I might be saying something as well.

1254
01:51:57,542 --> 01:51:59,567
First to the finish, then?

1255
01:52:20,865 --> 01:52:23,425
Do you fear death?

1256
01:52:24,969 --> 01:52:26,732
You've no idea.

1257
01:52:29,674 --> 01:52:31,539
Advise your Brethren,

1258
01:52:31,609 --> 01:52:34,703
you can fight, and all of you will die.

1259
01:52:34,779 --> 01:52:39,307
Or you can not fight,
in which case only most of you will die.

1260
01:52:39,884 --> 01:52:41,977
You murdered my father.

1261
01:52:43,354 --> 01:52:45,345
He chose his own fate.

1262
01:52:45,990 --> 01:52:48,720
And you have chosen yours.

1263
01:52:48,793 --> 01:52:50,852
We will fight.

1264
01:52:51,463 --> 01:52:53,260
And you will die.

1265
01:52:58,069 --> 01:52:59,696
So be it.

1266
01:53:05,710 --> 01:53:07,075
[Will] King?

1267
01:53:07,145 --> 01:53:09,978
Of the Brethren Court.
Courtesy of Jack.

1268
01:53:10,482 --> 01:53:12,814
Maybe he really
does know what he's doing.

1269
01:53:19,257 --> 01:53:20,246
Bravo!

1270
01:53:21,292 --> 01:53:25,490
You've successfully arrived aboard
the Dutchman as per the overall scheme.

1271
01:53:25,563 --> 01:53:27,292
- Look
- Oh, yes. Chapeau, mate.

1272
01:53:27,365 --> 01:53:30,425
Except for this sojourn in the brig,
it's utter clockwork.

1273
01:53:31,703 --> 01:53:32,863
Go away.

1274
01:53:32,937 --> 01:53:36,100
- What, back to the Locker?
- Not without you, Jackie.

1275
01:53:36,941 --> 01:53:38,033
Stab the heart.

1276
01:53:43,915 --> 01:53:49,046
Live forever as captain
of the Flying Dutchman.

1277
01:53:52,857 --> 01:53:54,449
Then again...

1278
01:53:55,293 --> 01:53:56,988
...if you're in the brig...

1279
01:53:58,496 --> 01:54:00,487
...who's to stab the heart?

1280
01:54:03,001 --> 01:54:05,868
Does seem to put immortality
a bit out of reach.

1281
01:54:07,605 --> 01:54:09,342
Peanut.

1282
01:54:09,342 --> 01:54:12,607
We'll need to use the Black Pearl
as a flagship to lead the attack.

1283
01:54:12,678 --> 01:54:16,842
- Oh, will we now?
- All right, Mrs. Fish. Come on.

1284
01:54:17,450 --> 01:54:20,886
Barbossa, you can't release her.

1285
01:54:20,953 --> 01:54:22,853
We need to give Jack a chance.

1286
01:54:23,456 --> 01:54:28,393
Apologies, Your Majesty. Too long
my fate has not been in me own hands.

1287
01:54:31,263 --> 01:54:32,560
No longer.

1288
01:54:34,567 --> 01:54:36,262
The enemy...

1289
01:54:37,436 --> 01:54:39,768
...has opted for oblivion.

1290
01:54:42,908 --> 01:54:44,535
Ready the fleet.

1291
01:54:44,777 --> 01:54:47,507
- To your stations.
- [man] To your stations.

1292
01:54:53,986 --> 01:54:57,149
Be there some manner
of rite or incantation?

1293
01:54:57,223 --> 01:54:58,485
Aye.

1294
01:54:58,924 --> 01:55:02,189
The items brought together, done.

1295
01:55:02,261 --> 01:55:04,661
Items to be burned...

1296
01:55:05,331 --> 01:55:07,492
...and someone must speak the words:

1297
01:55:07,566 --> 01:55:11,058
"Calypso, I release you
from your human bonds."

1298
01:55:11,137 --> 01:55:12,126
Is that it?

1299
01:55:12,705 --> 01:55:16,766
'Tis said it must be spoken
as if to a lover.

1300
01:55:16,842 --> 01:55:18,537
[all] Ooh!

1301
01:55:22,615 --> 01:55:27,917
Calypso, I release you
from your human bonds!

1302
01:55:37,496 --> 01:55:41,557
- Is that it?
- No, no, no. He didn't say it right.

1303
01:55:46,505 --> 01:55:47,529
He didn't...

1304
01:55:48,007 --> 01:55:49,872
You have to say it right.

1305
01:55:58,484 --> 01:55:59,473
Calypso...

1306
01:56:04,857 --> 01:56:07,291
...I release you from your human bonds.

1307
01:56:25,878 --> 01:56:27,573
Tia Dalma.

1308
01:56:30,015 --> 01:56:31,346
Calypso.

1309
01:56:33,819 --> 01:56:37,983
When the Brethren Court imprisoned you,
who was it that told them how?

1310
01:56:40,426 --> 01:56:44,328
- Who was it that betrayed you?
- Name him.

1311
01:56:45,598 --> 01:56:47,361
Davy Jones.

1312
01:57:01,380 --> 01:57:05,214
This is it! This is it!

1313
01:57:09,255 --> 01:57:10,415
[rope chafing]

1314
01:57:23,636 --> 01:57:25,501
[snapping]

1315
01:57:47,826 --> 01:57:49,589
Calypso!

1316
01:57:51,797 --> 01:57:56,928
I come before you as but a servant,
humble and contrite.

1317
01:57:58,837 --> 01:58:03,706
I have fulfilled me vow
and now ask your favor.

1318
01:58:06,745 --> 01:58:09,737
Spare meself, me ship, me crew...

1319
01:58:10,149 --> 01:58:14,609
...but unleash your fury upon those who
dare pretend themselves your masters...

1320
01:58:15,120 --> 01:58:16,280
...or mine.

1321
01:58:23,662 --> 01:58:27,189
[yelling indistinct incantation]

1322
01:59:04,069 --> 01:59:06,060
[wincing]

1323
01:59:14,213 --> 01:59:15,475
Is that it?

1324
01:59:18,217 --> 01:59:20,208
Why, she's no help at all.

1325
01:59:23,922 --> 01:59:25,219
What now?

1326
01:59:26,125 --> 01:59:27,387
Nothing.

1327
01:59:28,193 --> 01:59:31,128
Our final hope has failed us.

1328
01:59:32,264 --> 01:59:34,789
[wind gusting]

1329
01:59:58,590 --> 02:00:01,582
- It's not over.
- There's still a fight to be had.

1330
02:00:01,660 --> 02:00:05,596
We've an armada against us, and
with the Dutchman, there's no chance.

1331
02:00:05,664 --> 02:00:07,962
There's only a fool's chance.

1332
02:00:08,033 --> 02:00:10,797
Revenge won't bring
your father back, Miss Swann,

1333
02:00:10,869 --> 02:00:14,134
and it's not something
I'm intending to die for.

1334
02:00:19,111 --> 02:00:20,601
You're right.

1335
02:00:25,984 --> 02:00:27,952
Then what shall we die for?

1336
02:00:31,657 --> 02:00:33,056
You will listen to me.

1337
02:00:33,792 --> 02:00:35,054
Listen!

1338
02:00:37,996 --> 02:00:43,059
The Brethren will still be looking here
to us, to the Black Pearl, to lead.

1339
02:00:43,635 --> 02:00:45,068
And what will they see?

1340
02:00:45,571 --> 02:00:48,904
Frightened bilge rats
aboard a derelict ship? No.

1341
02:00:48,974 --> 02:00:53,206
No, they will see free men and freedom!

1342
02:00:53,278 --> 02:00:56,714
And what the enemy will see
is the flash of our cannons.

1343
02:00:56,782 --> 02:01:02,015
They will hear the ring of our swords,
and they will know what we can do.

1344
02:01:04,723 --> 02:01:07,021
By the sweat of our brows,

1345
02:01:07,092 --> 02:01:09,424
and the strength of our backs...

1346
02:01:09,828 --> 02:01:12,820
...and the courage of our hearts.

1347
02:01:15,534 --> 02:01:17,024
Gentlemen...

1348
02:01:19,738 --> 02:01:21,205
...hoist the colors.

1349
02:01:23,242 --> 02:01:24,675
Hoist the colors.

1350
02:01:24,743 --> 02:01:27,041
- Hoist the colors.
- Hoist the colors!

1351
02:01:27,112 --> 02:01:29,171
Aye.

1352
02:01:29,248 --> 02:01:32,911
The wind's on our side, boys.
That's all we need!

1353
02:01:32,985 --> 02:01:34,816
[shouting]

1354
02:01:34,887 --> 02:01:36,980
Hoist the colors!

1355
02:01:37,956 --> 02:01:39,947
[shouting in Chinese]

1356
02:02:28,040 --> 02:02:30,235
We have a favorable wind, sir.

1357
02:02:30,976 --> 02:02:32,170
Oh, so we do.

1358
02:02:32,811 --> 02:02:35,780
Signal Jones to give no quarter.

1359
02:02:36,815 --> 02:02:38,874
That should brighten his day.

1360
02:02:42,821 --> 02:02:46,757
To arms! We give no quarter!

1361
02:02:56,001 --> 02:02:57,798
Calypso.

1362
02:02:58,537 --> 02:03:01,062
- [thunder rumbling]
- [screeching]

1363
02:03:24,429 --> 02:03:27,364
Have you noticed,
on top of everything, it's raining?

1364
02:03:27,432 --> 02:03:28,490
That's a bad sign.

1365
02:03:28,567 --> 02:03:33,504
Man the capstan. Raise the main topyard.
Keep that powder dry.

1366
02:03:33,572 --> 02:03:36,234
[thunderclap]

1367
02:03:48,120 --> 02:03:49,951
Maelstrom!

1368
02:03:53,158 --> 02:03:55,183
[Elizabeth] Captain Barbossa!

1369
02:03:56,128 --> 02:03:58,153
We need you at the helm.

1370
02:04:00,666 --> 02:04:03,032
Aye, that be true.

1371
02:04:05,337 --> 02:04:10,297
Brace up yards,
you cack-handed deck apes.

1372
02:04:10,542 --> 02:04:14,444
Dying is the day worth living for.

1373
02:04:24,756 --> 02:04:26,383
Veer off!

1374
02:04:26,458 --> 02:04:28,722
She'll not harm us.

1375
02:04:29,227 --> 02:04:31,559
Full-bore and into the abyss.

1376
02:04:32,264 --> 02:04:33,595
Are you mad?

1377
02:04:33,665 --> 02:04:36,634
Ha! You afraid to get wet?

1378
02:04:46,178 --> 02:04:48,544
She's on our stern and gaining.

1379
02:04:48,613 --> 02:04:54,176
More speed!
Haul your wind and hold your water.

1380
02:05:01,159 --> 02:05:03,559
Bow cannons!

1381
02:05:04,062 --> 02:05:06,530
[man] Blow the cannons!

1382
02:05:17,409 --> 02:05:20,310
Take us out or they'll overbear us.

1383
02:05:20,379 --> 02:05:25,282
Nay. Further in.
We'll cut across to faster waters.

1384
02:05:26,485 --> 02:05:28,578
Prepare to broadside.

1385
02:05:39,031 --> 02:05:41,295
Captain the guns. Bear a hand.

1386
02:05:41,366 --> 02:05:45,200
Muster your courage, men.
At the ready.

1387
02:05:52,377 --> 02:05:55,972
Think like the whelp. Think like
the whelp. Think like the whelp.

1388
02:05:56,048 --> 02:05:58,243
Hinges. Hinges.

1389
02:05:58,316 --> 02:05:59,613
Think like the whelp.

1390
02:05:59,684 --> 02:06:01,117
Half-barrel hinges.

1391
02:06:01,219 --> 02:06:02,516
Leverage.

1392
02:06:07,492 --> 02:06:09,722
Wish us luck, boys. We'll need it.

1393
02:06:11,196 --> 02:06:14,029
- I miss him already.
- He is quite charming, isn't he?

1394
02:06:14,099 --> 02:06:15,657
Nobody move.

1395
02:06:16,735 --> 02:06:18,498
I dropped me brain.

1396
02:06:21,506 --> 02:06:23,497
[thunderclap]

1397
02:06:28,380 --> 02:06:31,144
Batten down the hatches!

1398
02:06:32,451 --> 02:06:34,476
Stand to your guns.

1399
02:06:35,053 --> 02:06:37,544
Midship cannoneers, sight the masts.

1400
02:06:41,793 --> 02:06:44,227
Main battery at the ready!

1401
02:06:48,800 --> 02:06:53,066
Hold there!
Wait till we're board-and-board.

1402
02:06:57,642 --> 02:06:59,371
Fire!

1403
02:06:59,744 --> 02:07:04,078
- Fire!
- Fire! Fire all!

1404
02:07:05,016 --> 02:07:06,176
Fire!

1405
02:07:10,055 --> 02:07:11,613
Fire!

1406
02:07:21,199 --> 02:07:22,666
Come on, men.

1407
02:07:32,677 --> 02:07:37,307
It be too late
to alter course now, mateys.

1408
02:07:41,686 --> 02:07:43,916
Halt there or we'll shoot.

1409
02:07:45,423 --> 02:07:46,549
Good one.

1410
02:07:49,027 --> 02:07:51,052
I just come to get me effects.

1411
02:07:52,664 --> 02:07:56,862
Admirable though it may be, why are
you here when you could be elsewhere?

1412
02:07:56,935 --> 02:07:59,199
Someone has to stay
and guard the chest.

1413
02:08:00,672 --> 02:08:05,234
There's no question, there's a breakdown
in military discipline on this vessel.

1414
02:08:05,310 --> 02:08:06,971
I blame the fish people.

1415
02:08:07,045 --> 02:08:11,414
Oh, so fish people,
by dint of being fish people,

1416
02:08:11,483 --> 02:08:14,714
automatically aren't
as disciplined as non-fish people?

1417
02:08:14,786 --> 02:08:17,152
Seems contributory,
is all I'm suggesting.

1418
02:08:17,222 --> 02:08:22,125
It's true, if there were no fish people,
there'd be no need to guard the chest.

1419
02:08:22,194 --> 02:08:25,630
And if there were no chest
we wouldn't need to be here to guard it.

1420
02:08:28,600 --> 02:08:31,592
[men shouting]

1421
02:08:33,171 --> 02:08:35,196
Prepare to board!

1422
02:08:36,841 --> 02:08:38,035
Cover!

1423
02:08:42,380 --> 02:08:44,871
- [groaning]
- [grunts]

1424
02:08:54,693 --> 02:08:56,422
No!

1425
02:09:03,768 --> 02:09:05,702
[chuckles]

1426
02:09:21,119 --> 02:09:22,279
[Jones chuckles]

1427
02:09:22,354 --> 02:09:27,314
Lookee here, boys. A lost bird.

1428
02:09:27,392 --> 02:09:31,294
A lost bird that never learned to fly.

1429
02:09:32,597 --> 02:09:34,360
To my great regret.

1430
02:09:35,667 --> 02:09:36,793
But...

1431
02:09:39,537 --> 02:09:41,528
...never too late to learn, eh?

1432
02:09:47,145 --> 02:09:49,636
[screaming]

1433
02:09:49,714 --> 02:09:51,181
Whoa!

1434
02:09:57,822 --> 02:10:01,155
The chest, hand it over.

1435
02:10:01,226 --> 02:10:03,057
I can set you free, mate.

1436
02:10:03,128 --> 02:10:06,154
My freedom was forfeit long ago.

1437
02:10:53,211 --> 02:10:54,542
Elizabeth.

1438
02:10:58,316 --> 02:10:59,544
Elizabeth.

1439
02:11:00,585 --> 02:11:01,950
Will you marry me?

1440
02:11:03,755 --> 02:11:05,586
I don't think now's the best time.

1441
02:11:09,394 --> 02:11:11,453
Now may be the only time.

1442
02:11:16,668 --> 02:11:17,862
I love you.

1443
02:11:23,808 --> 02:11:26,333
I've made my choice. What's yours?

1444
02:11:27,178 --> 02:11:28,475
Barbossa.

1445
02:11:29,948 --> 02:11:31,381
Marry us.

1446
02:11:32,484 --> 02:11:35,146
I'm a little busy at the moment.

1447
02:11:41,359 --> 02:11:43,350
Barbossa, now.

1448
02:11:45,029 --> 02:11:46,360
Fine, then.

1449
02:11:57,242 --> 02:12:00,370
Dearly beloved,
we be gathered here today...

1450
02:12:01,079 --> 02:12:04,606
To nail your gizzards
to the mast, you poxy cur.

1451
02:12:09,487 --> 02:12:12,513
Elizabeth Swann,
do you take me to be your husband?

1452
02:12:12,590 --> 02:12:14,558
- I do.
- Great.

1453
02:12:21,032 --> 02:12:23,296
Will Turner, do you take me...

1454
02:12:24,569 --> 02:12:26,298
...to be your wife...

1455
02:12:30,708 --> 02:12:32,676
...in sickness and in health...

1456
02:12:33,578 --> 02:12:35,808
...with health being the less likely?

1457
02:12:43,021 --> 02:12:44,249
I do.

1458
02:12:46,524 --> 02:12:49,425
As captain, I now pronounce you...

1459
02:12:52,397 --> 02:12:54,228
You may kiss...

1460
02:12:54,299 --> 02:12:55,698
[cackles]

1461
02:13:03,107 --> 02:13:04,540
You may kiss...

1462
02:13:09,514 --> 02:13:11,175
Just kiss.

1463
02:13:32,170 --> 02:13:33,569
[monkey screeches]

1464
02:13:49,888 --> 02:13:51,549
Leave it.

1465
02:14:03,801 --> 02:14:06,463
You can do nothing without the key.

1466
02:14:06,538 --> 02:14:08,005
I already have the key.

1467
02:14:09,274 --> 02:14:10,707
No, you don't.

1468
02:14:12,210 --> 02:14:13,507
Oh, that key.

1469
02:14:49,581 --> 02:14:50,570
No!

1470
02:15:17,642 --> 02:15:20,133
Oi. My pistol.

1471
02:15:27,385 --> 02:15:28,977
[screams]

1472
02:15:47,839 --> 02:15:49,238
Turner.

1473
02:15:54,245 --> 02:15:55,234
Fire!

1474
02:15:58,249 --> 02:16:01,116
[muffled grunting]

1475
02:16:01,185 --> 02:16:02,743
Thank you, Jack.

1476
02:16:32,583 --> 02:16:34,107
Stop! It's me.

1477
02:16:36,621 --> 02:16:39,522
It's Will. Your son.

1478
02:17:08,319 --> 02:17:09,684
Ha!

1479
02:17:25,336 --> 02:17:27,270
[screams]

1480
02:17:37,215 --> 02:17:38,614
- [thud]
- [both grunt]

1481
02:17:40,651 --> 02:17:42,118
Go!

1482
02:18:00,538 --> 02:18:04,599
Harridan! You'll see no mercy from me.

1483
02:18:04,675 --> 02:18:07,007
That's why I brought this.

1484
02:18:21,292 --> 02:18:22,850
[Elizabeth grunting]

1485
02:18:26,430 --> 02:18:29,661
I'm not going to kill you.
I made you a promise.

1486
02:18:36,407 --> 02:18:38,204
[yells]

1487
02:18:38,276 --> 02:18:39,607
Missed.

1488
02:18:40,812 --> 02:18:45,681
Did you forget?
I'm a heartless wretch.

1489
02:19:02,233 --> 02:19:05,532
Ah. Love.

1490
02:19:05,903 --> 02:19:09,395
A dreadful bond.

1491
02:19:10,174 --> 02:19:15,168
And yet, so easily severed.

1492
02:19:15,246 --> 02:19:20,445
Tell me, William Turner,
do you fear death?

1493
02:19:20,518 --> 02:19:21,951
[Jack] Do you?

1494
02:19:24,388 --> 02:19:26,720
[heart beating]

1495
02:19:32,697 --> 02:19:36,394
Heady tonic, holding life and death
in the palm of one's hand.

1496
02:19:37,001 --> 02:19:41,404
You're a cruel man, Jack Sparrow.

1497
02:19:42,273 --> 02:19:44,707
Cruel is a matter of perspective.

1498
02:19:45,276 --> 02:19:46,834
Is it?

1499
02:19:48,946 --> 02:19:50,743
- [groans]
- [gasps]

1500
02:20:12,603 --> 02:20:13,729
Will?

1501
02:20:15,339 --> 02:20:17,933
Look at me! Stay with me!
You're all right.

1502
02:20:23,814 --> 02:20:25,475
William?

1503
02:20:25,917 --> 02:20:27,612
My son.

1504
02:20:39,497 --> 02:20:42,989
Will? Will?
Look at me. Look at me!

1505
02:20:46,704 --> 02:20:50,606
You will not forestall my judgment.

1506
02:21:03,287 --> 02:21:04,948
[gasping]

1507
02:21:06,390 --> 02:21:08,051
Calypso.

1508
02:21:13,898 --> 02:21:19,359
She's taking us down! Make quick
or it's the Locker for us all!

1509
02:21:50,334 --> 02:21:51,392
No.

1510
02:21:55,506 --> 02:21:56,996
No, no.

1511
02:21:57,074 --> 02:21:58,803
No!

1512
02:22:00,077 --> 02:22:04,377
[all] Part of the crew.
Part of the ship, part of the crew.

1513
02:22:04,448 --> 02:22:07,940
Part of the ship, part of the crew.

1514
02:22:08,019 --> 02:22:10,044
Don't leave me.

1515
02:22:11,355 --> 02:22:13,050
Don't. No!

1516
02:22:13,124 --> 02:22:14,989
I won't leave you.

1517
02:22:25,102 --> 02:22:27,297
The Dutchman must have a captain.

1518
02:22:30,641 --> 02:22:32,632
Hold on.

1519
02:23:26,363 --> 02:23:28,024
Thank goodness, Jack.

1520
02:23:28,099 --> 02:23:32,058
The armada's still out there. The
Endeavor's coming up hard to starboard,

1521
02:23:32,136 --> 02:23:36,232
and I think it's time we embrace that
oldest and noblest of pirate traditions.

1522
02:23:36,307 --> 02:23:38,935
Never actually been one for tradition.

1523
02:23:43,914 --> 02:23:46,940
Close haul her.
Luff the sails and lay her in irons.

1524
02:23:47,017 --> 02:23:49,281
Belay that,
or we'll be a sitting duck.

1525
02:23:49,353 --> 02:23:50,684
Belay that "belay that."

1526
02:23:50,754 --> 02:23:52,517
- But the arma...
- Belay.

1527
02:23:52,623 --> 02:23:54,887
- The Endeav...
- Stow. Shut it.

1528
02:24:07,972 --> 02:24:09,997
What are they waiting for?

1529
02:24:10,975 --> 02:24:13,944
He expects us to honor our agreement.

1530
02:24:16,113 --> 02:24:18,946
[man] Ready guns and gun ports.

1531
02:24:22,686 --> 02:24:27,589
It's nothing personal, Jack.
It's just good business.

1532
02:24:54,451 --> 02:24:56,942
Ah, she survived.

1533
02:25:01,759 --> 02:25:04,091
[grunting and moaning]

1534
02:25:22,079 --> 02:25:25,446
- Ready on the guns.
- [all] Guns ready.

1535
02:25:26,083 --> 02:25:28,244
Full canvas.

1536
02:25:28,319 --> 02:25:30,685
Aye, full canvas.

1537
02:25:55,212 --> 02:25:56,509
Orders, sir.

1538
02:25:59,984 --> 02:26:01,110
Sir!

1539
02:26:01,752 --> 02:26:02,741
Cap'n.

1540
02:26:03,187 --> 02:26:05,815
- Fire.
- Fire!

1541
02:26:05,889 --> 02:26:07,948
- Fire!
- Fire!

1542
02:26:08,025 --> 02:26:09,993
Fire, all!

1543
02:26:20,571 --> 02:26:23,472
Orders? Orders, sir?

1544
02:26:35,853 --> 02:26:37,320
Sir, what do you command?

1545
02:26:37,955 --> 02:26:39,286
It's just...

1546
02:26:41,392 --> 02:26:43,087
...good business.

1547
02:26:44,795 --> 02:26:46,126
Abandon ship!

1548
02:26:46,196 --> 02:26:50,223
- Abandon ship!
- All hands, abandon ship!

1549
02:27:29,340 --> 02:27:32,173
- [glass shatters]
- [explosions]

1550
02:28:15,986 --> 02:28:17,715
They're turning away!

1551
02:28:17,788 --> 02:28:20,780
[shouting]

1552
02:28:30,067 --> 02:28:31,932
[squawks] Wind in your sails.

1553
02:28:37,174 --> 02:28:39,233
Shiver me timbers.

1554
02:28:48,685 --> 02:28:51,483
[shouting, cheering]

1555
02:29:00,230 --> 02:29:02,289
Captain Turner!

1556
02:29:16,947 --> 02:29:19,040
- Mr. Gibbs.
- Cap'n?

1557
02:29:20,250 --> 02:29:23,242
- You may throw my hat if you like.
- Aye, aye, cap'n.

1558
02:29:23,320 --> 02:29:25,015
Hooray!

1559
02:29:27,257 --> 02:29:29,020
Now go and get it.

1560
02:29:47,444 --> 02:29:49,036
Orders, sir?

1561
02:29:53,317 --> 02:29:57,344
You're no longer bound
to the Dutchman. You're free.

1562
02:29:57,421 --> 02:29:59,412
Aye.

1563
02:29:59,490 --> 02:30:01,890
That's a fine thing, but...

1564
02:30:03,494 --> 02:30:08,193
...by my reckoning,
I still have a debt that has to be paid.

1565
02:30:08,265 --> 02:30:09,857
If you'll have me.

1566
02:30:13,904 --> 02:30:15,769
On the wheel, then, Mr. Turner.

1567
02:30:17,140 --> 02:30:19,131
Aye, Captain Turner.

1568
02:30:34,091 --> 02:30:36,753
[Bootstrap Bill] This ship
has a purpose again.

1569
02:30:36,827 --> 02:30:38,852
And where we are bound...

1570
02:30:39,530 --> 02:30:41,998
...she cannot come.

1571
02:30:42,065 --> 02:30:44,033
One day ashore.

1572
02:30:44,101 --> 02:30:46,194
Ten years at sea.

1573
02:30:47,170 --> 02:30:50,003
That's a steep price
for what's been done.

1574
02:30:52,175 --> 02:30:54,166
Depends on the one day.

1575
02:30:56,947 --> 02:30:59,507
Your chariot awaits, Your Highness.

1576
02:31:03,320 --> 02:31:05,311
The oars are inside.

1577
02:31:16,934 --> 02:31:18,902
Mrs. Turner.

1578
02:31:23,807 --> 02:31:25,365
Goodbye, poppet.

1579
02:31:31,181 --> 02:31:32,478
Jack.

1580
02:31:33,717 --> 02:31:36,242
It would never
have worked out between us.

1581
02:31:39,590 --> 02:31:42,058
Keep telling yourself that, darling.

1582
02:31:45,495 --> 02:31:47,360
Once was quite enough.

1583
02:31:49,766 --> 02:31:51,757
Thank you.

1584
02:32:11,088 --> 02:32:13,079
I'm going to need the other one.

1585
02:32:45,088 --> 02:32:47,079
It's nearly sunset.

1586
02:33:03,540 --> 02:33:05,531
[heart beating]

1587
02:33:06,243 --> 02:33:07,938
It's always belonged to you.

1588
02:33:13,717 --> 02:33:15,275
Will you keep it safe?

1589
02:33:17,087 --> 02:33:18,247
Yes.

1590
02:33:22,259 --> 02:33:23,351
Yes.

1591
02:33:43,280 --> 02:33:44,269
Will!

1592
02:33:59,863 --> 02:34:02,024
Keep a weather eye on the horizon.

1593
02:34:30,894 --> 02:34:34,193
Granted it tends to list to port,
and has been, on occasion,

1594
02:34:34,264 --> 02:34:36,494
known to frighten young women.

1595
02:34:36,566 --> 02:34:40,935
But I promise you,
you will not be disappointed.

1596
02:34:41,638 --> 02:34:42,764
Is that it?

1597
02:34:43,373 --> 02:34:44,897
The Black Pearl?.

1598
02:34:45,542 --> 02:34:47,635
It's not very big.

1599
02:34:47,711 --> 02:34:50,111
Love, that is a dinghy.

1600
02:34:50,180 --> 02:34:55,550
My vessel is magnificent
and fierce and huge-ish and...

1601
02:34:56,019 --> 02:34:59,147
...gone. Why is it gone?

1602
02:35:00,090 --> 02:35:01,682
Is that it there?

1603
02:35:02,893 --> 02:35:04,019
Yes, there it is.

1604
02:35:05,028 --> 02:35:06,256
Why is it there?

1605
02:35:07,631 --> 02:35:09,189
It's much larger up close.

1606
02:35:09,266 --> 02:35:11,632
[sighs] Jack, you promised
to take us for a ride.

1607
02:35:11,701 --> 02:35:15,637
- I was to be given the first ride.
- What? You?

1608
02:35:15,705 --> 02:35:18,299
- Not what he said.
- [gasps] I never!

1609
02:35:20,110 --> 02:35:24,240
Mr. Gibbs? Any particular reason
why my ship is gone?

1610
02:35:24,314 --> 02:35:27,147
The ship? We're on the ship.

1611
02:35:29,786 --> 02:35:32,653
Jack! The ship's gone!

1612
02:35:32,722 --> 02:35:33,780
Really?

1613
02:35:35,826 --> 02:35:39,091
Ladies! Will you please shut it?

1614
02:35:39,162 --> 02:35:42,529
Listen to me.
Yes, I lied to you.

1615
02:35:42,599 --> 02:35:44,089
No, I don't love you.

1616
02:35:44,167 --> 02:35:47,261
Of course it makes you look fat.
I've never been to Brussels.

1617
02:35:47,337 --> 02:35:50,067
It is pronounced "egregious."

1618
02:35:50,140 --> 02:35:54,167
By the way, no, I've never actually
met Pizarro, but I love his pies.

1619
02:35:54,244 --> 02:35:56,940
And all of this pales
to utter insignificance

1620
02:35:57,013 --> 02:36:00,744
in light of the fact that
my ship is once again gone. Savvy?

1621
02:36:13,296 --> 02:36:16,231
- Take what you can.
- Give nothing back.

1622
02:36:20,136 --> 02:36:24,163
Tell me, ladies,
what do you know of sea turtles?

1623
02:36:24,241 --> 02:36:25,333
Sea turtles?

1624
02:36:25,609 --> 02:36:27,839
Let me tell you
the amazing story of Cap...

1625
02:36:27,911 --> 02:36:30,573
[Barbossa] All men up the ratlines.

1626
02:36:30,981 --> 02:36:33,779
All men up the ratlines!

1627
02:36:38,922 --> 02:36:41,152
Oh, you want a taste of this?

1628
02:36:42,659 --> 02:36:45,856
Oh, that's a good boy!
You're Daddy's boy.

1629
02:36:45,929 --> 02:36:48,557
Oh, yes, you are.
Oh, yes, you are.

1630
02:36:48,632 --> 02:36:49,621
Sir?

1631
02:36:51,001 --> 02:36:53,526
Some of the men don't feel
entirely settled

1632
02:36:53,603 --> 02:36:56,094
- about leaving Captain Jack behind.
- Again.

1633
02:36:56,239 --> 02:36:59,140
- Again.
- Is that so?

1634
02:36:59,409 --> 02:37:03,072
It would make us feel a whole lot
better regarding our fortunes

1635
02:37:03,146 --> 02:37:05,842
if we could see that item
you told us about.

1636
02:37:05,916 --> 02:37:08,077
- On the charts.
- Aye.

1637
02:37:08,184 --> 02:37:09,583
With our own eyes.

1638
02:37:09,653 --> 02:37:13,248
To help put an ease
to our burden of guilt, so to speak.

1639
02:37:13,690 --> 02:37:15,521
Ah.

1640
02:37:15,592 --> 02:37:17,822
Feast your eyes upon this, mateys.

1641
02:37:18,228 --> 02:37:20,753
There's more than one way
to live forever.

1642
02:37:20,830 --> 02:37:24,561
Gents, I give you
the Fountain of Youth.

1643
02:37:35,345 --> 02:37:37,210
Sparrow.

1644
02:37:37,714 --> 02:37:42,276
[Jack] * Yo ho, yo ho
A pirate's life for me

1645
02:37:42,352 --> 02:37:44,946
[humming]

1646
02:38:08,178 --> 02:38:12,808
 We're devils and black sheep
Really bad eggs

1647
02:38:31,901 --> 02:38:34,529
 Drink up, me hearties
Yo ho

1648
02:47:06,949 --> 02:47:11,818
[boy] * Yo ho, yo ho
A pirate's life for me

1649
02:47:11,888 --> 02:47:14,982
 We pillage, we plunder
We rifle and loot

1650
02:47:15,057 --> 02:47:16,854
 Drink up, me hearties
Yo ho

1651
02:47:16,926 --> 02:47:19,554
 We kidnap and ravage
And don't give a hoot

1652
02:47:19,629 --> 02:47:21,460
 Drink up, me hearties
Yo ho

1653
02:47:21,531 --> 02:47:27,128
 Yo ho, yo ho
A pirate's life for me *

